window.FINAL_EXAM = {"questions":70,"rows":[{"id":"childhood","title":"Childhood, Neurodevelopmental & Impulse Control Disorders","chapters":"Chapters 11 & 21","questions":13,"week":6,"isNew":true,"meds":"in","focus":["Pediatric medications: dextroamphetamine/amphetamine, methylphenidate, atomoxetine, bupropion, clonidine, guanfacine, imipramine","The two stimulant classes and the difference between them: methylphenidate and dextroamphetamine/amphetamine","Age limits: certain stimulants are not used until age 6","All other childhood, neurodevelopmental and impulse control content"],"out":["Methylphenidate trade names and specific formulations: Daytrana, Methylin, Ritalin, Ritalin LA, Concerta"],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Childhood Mental Health","section":"childhood","page":"week6-childhood-mental-health.html","pageTitle":"Childhood Mental Health","terms":["mt4dhk","1ohhwey"],"bullets":[{"s":1,"h":"<b>Identify the most common childhood psychiatric disorders.</b> In children 8 to 15: <b>ADHD is the most common</b>, then <b>mood disorders</b> (major depression the largest), then <b>conduct disorder</b>, dysthymia, anxiety disorders, panic disorder and GAD, and eating disorders least."},{"h":"<b>Examine the risk factors for mental illness in children.</b> <b>Biological</b>: genetic predisposition, neurobiological, prenatal or postnatal factors. <b>Psychological</b>: <b>temperament</b>. <b>Environmental</b>: <b>abuse or trauma, low socioeconomic status</b>, maternal psychiatric disorders and <b>parenting</b>."},{"h":"<b>Identify the factors that promote resilience (protective factors).</b> <b>Positive self-image</b>, <b>family cohesion</b> and absence of discord, <b>a positive relationship with at least one parent</b>, positive early family experiences, <b>support</b>, <b>academic achievement</b>, <b>positive peer relationships</b> and <b>temperament</b>."},{"h":"<b>Apply the nursing process to assessment: the interview.</b> The <b>clinical interview</b> is the primary tool. Same format as adults, except <b>simple, concrete phrases</b>, <b>direct questions rather than open-ended</b>, <b>corroborating with the adult</b>, play media, and a timeline that may be inaccurate. <b>Children report internal symptoms better</b> (mood, sleep, suicidal ideation), <b>parents external ones</b> (behavior, relationships). The assessment covers family functioning, the current problem, history, mental status and a physical exam."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Conduct the interview by developmental level.</b> <b>Preschool</b>: difficulty putting feelings into words, so <b>use play</b> in a playroom. <b>School-age</b>: give longer explanations, so <b>establish rapport through competitive games</b>. <b>Adolescents</b>: egocentric and afraid of being shamed, so <b>tell them what will be shared with parents</b> with a <b>direct, candid approach</b>."},{"h":"<b>Apply the nursing process to interventions.</b> <b>Behavioral interventions</b> (reward close to the behavior for young children), <b>bibliotherapy</b>, <b>expressive arts</b> and journaling, music therapy, <b>family interventions</b>, psychopharmacology, <b>disruptive behavior management</b> (least restrictive first) and <b>play therapy</b>. <b>Seclusion and restraint are a last resort</b>, only for dangerous behavior, with <b>continuous monitoring</b>, <b>never as punishment</b>, and <b>parents or guardians are notified</b> of any PRN, hold, seclusion or restraint."}]},{"label":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","section":"neurodevelopmental","page":"week6-neurodevelopmental-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders","terms":["be3yxh","915oky","yfzuuk","150vp65"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Examine the risk factors for autism and ADHD.</b> <b>Autism</b>: advanced parental age, spontaneous genetic mutations, <b>low birth weight or prematurity</b>, <b>valproic acid in pregnancy</b>, and genetic disorders such as fragile X and Rett syndrome. <b>No substantiated link to vaccines.</b> <b>ADHD</b>: genetic, low birth weight or prematurity, <b>frontal head injury</b>, <b>smoking or drinking in pregnancy</b>, and <b>lead or pesticide exposure</b>."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of the communication, motor and learning disorders.</b> <b>Speech disorders</b>: problems making sounds. <b>Language disorders</b>: difficulty using words in context, such as failing to follow directions. <b>Developmental coordination disorder</b>: coordination below developmental age. <b>Stereotypic movement disorder</b>: repetitive, purposeless movements <b>for 4 weeks or more</b>. <b>Specific learning disorder</b>: dyslexia, dyscalculia or dysgraphia, identified in the school years."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of autism spectrum disorder.</b> Symptoms in <b>socialization, communication and behavior</b>: withdrawal, <b>repetitive movements</b> such as hand flapping and rocking, <b>fixed interests</b>, <b>insistence on routine</b> (disruption can trigger self-harm), <b>poor sensory integration</b>, delayed language and <b>pronoun reversal</b>. Onset in the <b>first 3 years</b>, more common in males but <b>more severe in females</b>."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of the three types of ADHD.</b> <b>Hyperactivity-impulsivity</b>: fidgets, can't stay seated, \"driven by a motor,\" talks excessively, <b>blurts answers</b>, interrupts, can't wait a turn. <b>Inattentive</b>: careless mistakes, <b>easily distracted</b>, doesn't seem to listen, doesn't finish tasks, disorganized, <b>loses things</b>. Each needs <b>six or more criteria for 6 months</b>. <b>Combined</b> has both."},{"h":"<b>Describe the treatment of tic disorders, IDD and autism.</b> <b>Tics</b>: habit reversal, <b>relaxation strategies</b>, medications (antipsychotics, clonidine, clonazepam, fluoxetine, sertraline), and <b>DBS</b> when conservative approaches fail. <b>IDD</b>: early intervention, <b>social skills training</b> and individual, family and cognitive therapy. <b>Autism</b>: structured programs that build on the child's interests with a <b>predictable schedule</b>, <b>simple steps</b> and <b>regular positive reinforcement</b>, <b>parent involvement</b>, social skills and speech therapy, and <b>atypical antipsychotics, SSRIs and stimulants</b>."},{"h":"<b>Apply the nursing process to IDD and autism.</b> <b>IDD</b>: assess for <b>delays and signs of neglect or abuse</b>. Priority problems: <b>risk for injury</b>, ineffective impulse control, impaired verbal communication. Outcomes include <b>no self-harm</b>. <b>Autism</b>: <b>early identification is key</b>, with a multidisciplinary team, <b>hearing and lead screening</b> (lead poisoning can mimic autism), and the <b>CARS and M-CHAT</b> screening tools."},{"h":"<b>Describe the treatment of ADHD.</b> <b>Behavioral management plus FDA-approved medication gives the best results.</b> <b>Parent management training</b>, problem-solving and coping skills, and <b>group therapy of 8 to 12 weekly sessions</b>. <b>Play therapy and CBT are not as effective.</b> Teach a schedule, organizers, <b>specific, clear, consistent directions</b>, praise, and <b>small attainable goals</b>. <b>Stimulants are first line</b>, non-stimulants second."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Identify the two stimulant classes.</b> <b>methylphenidate</b> and <b>dextroamphetamine/amphetamine</b>. dextroamphetamine/amphetamine is approved for children <b>over age 6</b>. Stimulants increase dopamine, start low, and are <b>not weight dependent</b>."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Identify the side effects and monitoring of stimulants.</b> <b>Decreased appetite</b>, headaches, stomachaches, <b>trouble sleeping</b>, jitteriness, social withdrawal, <b>tachycardia or bradycardia</b>, <b>hypertension</b>, dry mouth, diarrhea. Manage by <b>adjusting the dose or timing</b>. A <b>dull, overly restricted child</b> needs a lower dose or a different drug. Monitor <b>heart rate, blood pressure, weight</b> and <b>random urine drug screens</b>. <b>Drug holidays</b> give the body a break."},{"h":"<b>Identify the non-stimulants for ADHD.</b> Second line, for intolerable side effects or a risk of abuse or diversion. <b>atomoxetine</b> (SNRI): slow response, over age 6, <b>especially with anxiety</b>, and <b>observe closely for suicidal ideation</b>. <b>bupropion</b> (NDRI): good for the <b>combined type</b>. <b>clonidine</b> (alpha agonist): <b>especially with tics</b>, and its side effects resolve after several doses. <b>guanfacine</b>: extended release, <b>at bedtime</b>. <b>imipramine</b> (TCA)."}]},{"label":"Impulse Control Disorders","section":"impulse-control","page":"week6-impulse-control-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Impulse Control Disorders","terms":["10xyfxe","1fp117g"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Examine the risk factors for the impulse control disorders.</b> <b>ODD</b>: genetic and family history, neurobiological causes, <b>family dysfunction and adverse childhood experiences</b>, temperament. <b>Conduct disorder</b>: <b>physical and sexual abuse</b>, <b>inconsistent parenting with harsh discipline</b>, lack of supervision, early institutional living or out-of-home placement, <b>a delinquent peer group</b>, parental substance abuse, and biologic factors. <b>Intermittent explosive disorder</b>: neurobiological abnormalities and <b>conflict or violence in the family of origin</b>."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of oppositional defiant disorder.</b> <b>Negative, hostile, defiant and vindictive</b>, with an <b>irritable, angry mood</b>, swearing, <b>mood lability</b>, <b>low frustration tolerance</b> (cannot tolerate being told no), argumentativeness and <b>blaming others</b>. The child <b>does not see themselves as angry or oppositional</b>, yet recognizes that others have rights and that there are rules."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of conduct disorder.</b> More severe than ODD, with onset in <b>late childhood or early adolescence</b>. <b>Lacks empathy</b> and guilt, with <b>remorse only at being caught</b>. <b>Cruelty to animals</b>, aggression toward people, <b>destruction of property</b>, pyromania and kleptomania, risk taking, and <b>persistent violation of others' rights</b>. <b>Childhood onset, before 10, has the worse prognosis.</b>"},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of intermittent explosive disorder.</b> <b>Inability to control aggressive impulses</b>, with <b>mean onset at 13 to 21</b>. The cycle runs <b>tension and arousal, explosive behavior, relief and release, then remorse</b>. It damages relationships, work and legal standing. Comorbid with <b>depressive, anxiety and substance use disorders</b> and <b>antisocial and borderline personality disorders</b>."},{"h":"<b>Describe the treatment of the impulse control disorders.</b> <b>ODD</b>: <b>parent management training</b>, group therapy, anger management, and <b>divalproex sodium</b> for anger and aggression, though <b>no drug is FDA-approved</b>. <b>Conduct disorder</b>: <b>six medication classes</b> treat symptoms (antidepressants, mood stabilizers, stimulants, antipsychotics, anticonvulsants, adrenergic medications), plus family supportive training, anger management and parent management training. <b>Intermittent explosive disorder</b>: <b>SSRIs</b>, mood stabilizers, antipsychotics and beta-blockers, with CBT and anger management. <b>Never benzodiazepines</b>, which reduce inhibitions."},{"h":"<b>Apply the nursing process to the impulse control disorders.</b> <b>Continually assess suicide risk and risk of violence toward others</b>, and focus nursing diagnoses on <b>protecting self and others from impulsive and premeditated acts</b>. <b>Promote safety</b>, establish rapport, <b>set limits and expectations</b>, <b>consistently follow through with consequences</b>, and provide <b>structure and boundaries</b>. With families: <b>make the home safe from weapons and drugs</b>, teach behavior modification, set realistic goals, and advocate for special education services."}]}],"roster":[["methylphenidate<br> dextroamphetamine/amphetamine","Stimulant","ADHD, first line."],["atomoxetine","SNRI, non-stimulant","ADHD, especially with anxiety."],["bupropion","NDRI, non-stimulant","ADHD, combined type."],["clonidine","Alpha agonist, non-stimulant","ADHD, especially with tics."],["guanfacine","Non-stimulant","ADHD hyperactivity and restlessness."],["imipramine","TCA, non-stimulant","ADHD."]],"rosterHref":"medications.html#w6-glance","medSections":[{"id":"w6-stimulants","title":"Stimulants for ADHD","html":"<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>ADHD, first line.</b> They <b>improve attention and focus</b> and <b>decrease hyperactivity</b> by increasing dopamine, a <b>paradoxical calming effect</b> in ADHD. <b>At least 80% of children respond to one</b>, so a second or third stimulant is tried before a non-stimulant. <b>dextroamphetamine/amphetamine and Concerta are approved only over age 6.</b></td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>Decreased appetite</b>, headaches, stomachaches, <b>trouble getting to sleep</b>, jitteriness, social withdrawal, nervousness, overstimulation, <b>tachycardia or bradycardia</b>, <b>hypertension</b>, restlessness, dry mouth, unpleasant taste, diarrhea. Usually managed by <b>adjusting the dose or when it is given</b>. A child who seems <b>dull or overly restricted</b>: <b>decrease the dose or change medication</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>Start low and work up.</b> <b>Not weight dependent.</b> <b>Long-acting forms last 8 to 12 hours</b>, taken once a day, useful when a school dose cannot be given. Each visit: <b>heart rate, blood pressure, weight</b> and a <b>random urine drug screen</b>. A negative screen means the stimulant is <b>no longer prescribed</b>, because of abuse and diversion. <b>Drug holidays</b> on weekends, holidays and vacations counteract side effects.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"},{"id":"w6-nonstimulants","title":"Non-Stimulants for ADHD","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>atomoxetine</b>, an SNRI</li>\n      <li><b>bupropion</b>, an NDRI</li>\n      <li><b>clonidine</b>, an alpha agonist</li>\n      <li><b>guanfacine</b>, extended release (Intuniv)</li>\n      <li><b>imipramine</b>, a TCA</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>ADHD, second line</b>, when stimulant side effects are not tolerated or there is a question of <b>abuse or diversion</b>. <b>atomoxetine</b>: children <b>over 6</b>, <b>especially with anxiety</b>, and it also helps ODD. <b>bupropion</b>: inattention, impulsivity and hyperactivity, good for the <b>combined type</b>. <b>clonidine</b>: alone or with a stimulant, <b>especially when tics are present</b>. <b>guanfacine</b>: hyperactivity, difficulty focusing and restlessness.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>atomoxetine</b>: dry mouth, dizziness, nausea and vomiting, decreased appetite, trouble sleeping. <b>bupropion</b>: dry mouth, dizziness, nausea, appetite changes, stomach pain, headache, ringing in the ears, sore throat, muscle pain. <b>clonidine</b>: dry mouth, dizziness, mild sedation, constipation, which <b>usually resolve after several doses</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>atomoxetine has a slow therapeutic response</b>, so teach that it takes time to work. <b>Observe closely for suicidal ideation</b> on atomoxetine and teach parents the warning signs. <b>guanfacine is usually taken at bedtime.</b></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-danger\"><b>atomoxetine: observe closely for suicidal ideation</b>, especially when it is started, and teach parents the warning signs of suicide.</div>"}],"lineups":["odd-ied-conduct","adhd-types"],"refs":["speech-language"]},{"id":"neurocognitive","title":"Neurocognitive Disorders & Older Adults","chapters":"Chapters 23 & 31","questions":9,"week":7,"isNew":true,"meds":"in","focus":["Delirium compared with dementia","The key terms, well enough to apply them","The stages of Alzheimer's disease, mild, moderate and severe, and what to do in each (textbook Table 23.3)","Memory deficit in normal aging compared with dementia (textbook Table 23.2)","The two medication classes for dementia: one works on acetylcholine, the other on glutamate","Older adults: textbook Chapter 31"],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Neurocognitive Disorders","section":"neurocognitive","page":"week7-neurocognitive-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Neurocognitive Disorders","terms":["1wr8hyc","1d2n3in","15m74dj","e7wh0l","wm0ndi","7y2q31","2s16e2","81o59u","1c1r1qt","10aopmt","3i6li6"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Compare and contrast the clinical picture of delirium with that of dementia.</b> <b>Delirium</b>: <b>acute onset over hours to a few days</b>, a <b>disturbance in attention, awareness and consciousness</b> that <b>fluctuates with periods of lucidity</b>, visual hallucinations and illusions, and always <b>a direct physiological cause</b>. A medical emergency. <b>Dementia</b>: <b>progressive deterioration</b> of cognitive functioning with <b>no change in consciousness</b>, affecting memory, orientation, vocabulary, calculation and abstract thinking."},{"h":"<b>Define neurocognitive disorders.</b> The DSM-5 categories are <b>delirium, mild and major neurocognitive disorder</b>. The condition is <b>acquired, not developmental</b>. <b>Mild</b>: a modest decline that <b>does not interfere with ADLs</b> and does not necessarily progress. <b>Major</b>: a significant decline that <b>interferes with daily functioning and independence</b>. <b>Alzheimer's disease is 60% to 80% of all dementias.</b>"},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Describe the pharmacological management of neurocognitive disorders.</b> <b>Delirium</b>: treat the underlying cause, with <b>very small doses of antipsychotics</b>, or <b>lorazepam</b> when there is hepatic dysfunction, and <b>mirtazapine</b> for sleep. <b>Alzheimer's disease</b>: <b>cholinesterase inhibitors</b> (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) tried first, then the <b>NMDA antagonist memantine</b>. They <b>delay, not decrease, cognitive decline</b>. Medications for behavioral symptoms are <b>off label</b>, and <b>antipsychotics may increase mortality</b>: nonpharmacological interventions first, then the smallest dose for the shortest time."},{"h":"<b>Explain the relationship among anticholinergic medications, acetylcholine and acetylcholinesterase inhibitors.</b> <b>Acetylcholine</b> is involved with learning, memory and mood, and <b>the brain produces less of it as Alzheimer's disease progresses</b>. <b>Cholinesterase inhibitors keep the enzyme from breaking it down</b>, raising its level and duration. More acetylcholine throughout the body explains the nausea, vomiting and bradycardia. <b>Anticholinergics are among the medications that cause delirium.</b>"},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Apply the nursing process to the care of a patient with a neurocognitive disorder.</b> <b>Delirium</b>: the first outcome is that the <b>patient remains safe and free from injury</b>. Give frequent, brief verbal orientation, <b>approach slowly from the front</b>, keep staff consistent, provide adequate lighting, clocks and calendars, and protect sleep. <b>Dementia</b>: priorities change by stage, from <b>delaying cognitive decline</b>, to <b>protecting the patient from self-harm</b>, to <b>physical needs</b>. Use short, simple sentences, <b>say what to do rather than what not to do</b>, and do not rely on the patient verbalizing pain."}]}],"roster":[["donepezil<br> rivastigmine","Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor","Alzheimer's disease, all stages."],["galantamine","Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor","Alzheimer's disease, mild to moderate."],["memantine","NMDA antagonist","Moderate to severe dementia."]],"rosterHref":"medications.html#w7-glance","medSections":[{"id":"w7-achei","title":"Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>donepezil</b></li>\n      <li><b>rivastigmine</b>, PO and transdermal patch</li>\n      <li><b>galantamine</b></li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Alzheimer's disease</b>, usually the class tried first. <b>galantamine</b>: mild to moderate. <b>donepezil and rivastigmine</b>: all stages. They <b>stop the breakdown of acetylcholine</b>, and are used to <b>delay, not decrease, cognitive decline</b>, stabilizing memory, language and orientation. <b>Peak in 3 months</b> but continue to delay decline. donepezil delays progression about 6 to 12 months.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>Nausea and vomiting</b> are the most common: <b>take with food</b>. Dizziness, headache, insomnia. The patch has <b>fewer GI effects</b> but can cause <b>skin irritation</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td>Rarely <b>bradycardia and syncope</b>, from the cholinergic effect. The risk of side effects <b>doubles in people over 85</b> after more than a year of use.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td>There is no cure, so the goal is slowing progression. <b>Minimal benefit after one year</b>: weigh side effects against benefit, quality of life and the client's wishes. <b>The rivastigmine patch is changed daily.</b> Stop it if irritation spreads beyond the size of the patch. Monitor for bradycardia and syncope.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"},{"id":"w7-nmda","title":"NMDA Antagonist","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>memantine</b></li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Moderate to severe dementia.</b> Used in all stages of Alzheimer's disease, and in vascular dementia. <b>Blocks glutamate at the NMDA receptor</b>, preventing the excess calcium movement into neurons that damages them. Slows the decline and can slightly improve cognitive functioning.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td>Mild: <b>dizziness, confusion, headaches and constipation</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td>Tried as the disease progresses or when a cholinesterase inhibitor has not worked well. <b>The two classes are sometimes given together.</b></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"}],"lineups":["delirium-dementia","aging-or-alzheimers"],"refs":["alzheimer-stages"]},{"id":"somatic","title":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","chapters":"Chapter 17","questions":8,"week":6,"isNew":true,"meds":"none","focus":[],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","section":"somatic","page":"week6-somatic-symptom-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Somatic Symptom Disorders","terms":["1f8cf7i","1rjfhp0","5wbwg0","1k3oaww"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations and impact of somatic symptom disorder.</b> <b>Actual, persistent, distressing physical symptoms that cause significant anxiety</b>, often <b>without a clear medical cause</b>, with preoccupation with health. Impact: chronic health anxiety, <b>frequent doctor visits and unnecessary tests</b>, impaired social and occupational functioning."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations and impact of illness anxiety disorder.</b> Intense <b>fear of having or acquiring a serious illness despite few or no actual symptoms</b>, with body checking and <b>reassurance seeking despite normal results</b>. Impact: avoids activities or places, and either <b>doctor shopping</b> or <b>avoiding healthcare altogether</b>. Unlike somatic symptom disorder, the focus is the fear, not actual symptoms."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations and impact of conversion disorder.</b> <b>Neurological symptoms</b> (paralysis, tremors, non-epileptic seizures, vision problems) <b>that medical tests cannot explain</b>, often <b>triggered by stress or trauma</b>. Impact: <b>sudden disability</b>, impaired mobility and independence, emotional distress."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations and impact of psychological factors affecting medical condition.</b> <b>Stress, anxiety or depression that worsens a physical medical condition</b> such as chronic pain, asthma or heart disease. Impact: <b>poor health outcomes</b> and impaired quality of life."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations and impact of factitious disorder, imposed on self or others.</b> <b>Intentionally faking or producing symptoms to assume the sick role</b> and gain attention or sympathy, an <b>internal incentive (primary gain)</b>. May falsify histories, manipulate test results or self-harm. Impact: trust issues and <b>unnecessary treatments and complications</b>."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations and impact of malingering.</b> <b>Intentionally exaggerating or fabricating symptoms for external gain (secondary gain)</b>, which is what separates it from factitious disorder. Impact: distrust with healthcare providers, <b>legal consequences</b> if fraud is detected, unnecessary treatments or accommodations."},{"h":"<b>Identify specific treatments for somatic symptom disorders.</b> <b>CBT is the primary and most effective treatment.</b> Also mindfulness and stress reduction, <b>psychoeducation in every disorder</b>, and interprofessional care. <b>Antidepressants and anxiolytics are not the primary intervention</b>, used mostly for co-occurring anxiety or depression. <b>Conversion disorder adds physical therapy and rehabilitation.</b> Factitious disorder: long-term psychotherapy, and family therapy if imposed on others. Malingering: psychiatric evaluation, then legal or social intervention."},{"h":"<b>Identify recommended nursing interventions for patients with somatic symptom disorders.</b> <b>Establish a therapeutic relationship</b> with trust and empathy. <b>Reduce health dependency</b> by coordinating appointments and a structured follow-up plan, with both medical and psychiatric care. Educate on the disorder, encourage realistic expectations, <b>teach coping strategies</b> (relaxation, meditation, biofeedback) and assist with medication management."}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":["somatic-six"],"refs":["somatic-primary-feature"]},{"id":"medical","title":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","chapters":"","questions":6,"week":7,"isNew":true,"meds":"none","focus":[],"out":["Dying, death and grieving (Chapter 30)"],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","section":"medical-mimics","page":"week7-medical-illnesses-psychiatric-symptoms.html","pageTitle":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms","terms":["czqi20","tzees6","1npx6k","6smd87"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Recognize psychiatric side effects (PSEs) from pharmacological treatment.</b> They <b>often resemble spontaneous psychiatric syndromes</b>, occur with <b>usual doses, intoxication or withdrawal</b>, and range from short-lasting anxiety to severe confusion, and suicide. Common offenders: <b>corticosteroids</b> (mood changes, psychosis), <b>beta blockers</b> and antiepileptics (delirium, psychosis), <b>anticholinergics</b> (delirium), <b>dopaminergic anti-Parkinson agents</b> and psychostimulants (psychosis, agitation), interferon (depression), benzodiazepine withdrawal (anxiety, agitation)."},{"h":"<b>Identify risk factors for PSEs.</b> <b>Polypharmacy</b>, <b>high doses</b>, route and <b>faster administration</b>, a <b>narrow therapeutic index</b>, metabolic conditions such as <b>hepatic insufficiency or slow metabolism</b>, augmented permeability of the blood-brain barrier, the <b>very young or elderly</b>, and postpartum or other stress such as the ICU."},{"h":"<b>Identify hallmark risk factors for an underlying medical mimic.</b> One or more atypical features: <b>normal functioning before a sudden onset</b>, <b>late onset</b>, a known medical condition, atypical presentation, <b>disturbance of gait or balance</b>, <b>visual, olfactory or tactile hallucinations</b>, and illicit or recent substance use. Also abnormal vital signs, no personal or family psychiatric history, family concern, and <b>getting worse after an antipsychotic or anxiolytic</b>."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Recognize the mental disorders due to a medical condition: mood, anxiety and psychotic.</b> <b>Cushing's disease and hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism mimic all three.</b> Mood: also sleep apnea, mononucleosis, CVA, B12 deficiency, dementia. Psychotic: also estrogen withdrawal, hypoglycemia, epilepsy, brain tumors. Anxiety: also cardiac arrhythmia, pulmonary embolism, hypoxia, COPD, CHF. <b>Hypothyroidism is very commonly confused with depression.</b>"},{"h":"<b>Describe nursing interventions for clients with a medical illness.</b> <b>Perform an accurate, thorough health history and physical exam on all clients.</b> <b>Ask everyone about substance use.</b> Review current prescriptions, OTC and alternative medications and recent changes. <b>Monitor labs and report abnormal labs immediately.</b>"},{"h":"<b>Describe nursing implications and interventions for clients with a mental disorder due to a medical condition.</b> A client is a <b>holistic entity</b>. Be aware that <b>common psychiatric symptoms often arise from medical illnesses</b>, and treat symptoms as <b>medically caused until proven otherwise</b>. Emotional dimensions may be overlooked in med-surg or primary care. Provide reassurance and support to families and clients."}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":[],"refs":[]},{"id":"trauma","title":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","chapters":"Chapter 16","questions":5,"week":5,"isNew":false,"meds":"out","focus":["Depersonalization","Derealization","Trauma-informed care","Adjustment disorder","Posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD)","Acute stress disorder","Depersonalization/derealization disorder","Treatments other than medications, including trauma-informed care"],"out":["Reactive attachment disorder and disinhibited social engagement disorder"],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","section":"trauma-stressor","page":"week5-trauma-stressor-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders","terms":["8qp706","123e3pi","1cx4a8j","1v57aew","1yb9q8h"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Describe the risk factors, timeframe and symptoms of acute stress disorder.</b> Diagnosed <b>3 days to 1 month</b> after the event, and <b>beyond 1 month the diagnosis becomes PTSD</b>. Requires <b>9 or more symptoms from any of five categories</b>: <b>intrusion, dissociative, negative mood, avoidance</b> and <b>arousal</b>, with <b>significant distress or impaired social or occupational functioning</b>, <b>not attributable to a substance or another medical disorder</b>. The <b>diagnostic emphasis on dissociative symptoms</b> separates it from PTSD."},{"h":"<b>Describe the risk factors, timeframe and symptoms of PTSD.</b> <b>Symptoms longer than 1 month</b>, severe enough to <b>interfere with daily life</b>, and <b>unrelated to medication, substance use or another illness</b>. Four symptom groups with required counts: <b>at least 1 re-experiencing, at least 1 avoidance, at least 2 arousal and reactivity, at least 2 cognition and mood</b>. Risk factors: <b>previous trauma especially in childhood, being hurt or seeing others hurt or killed, horror or helplessness, little social support afterward, extra stress afterward</b>, and a <b>personal or family history of mental illness or substance use</b>."},{"h":"<b>Identify the psychological treatment modalities for acute stress disorder and PTSD.</b> <b>Psychotherapy is the main treatment for acute stress disorder</b> and <b>trauma-focused psychotherapy is primary for PTSD</b>: <b>prolonged exposure, cognitive processing therapy</b> and <b>EMDR</b>. <b>Trauma-focused CBT has four components</b>: learning how the body responds to trauma, symptom management, reframing problematic thinking, and exposure therapy."},{"h":"<b>Describe depersonalization/derealization disorder in terms of symptoms, comorbidity, risk factors and treatments.</b> <b>Persistent or recurrent episodes of depersonalization, derealization or both</b>, lasting <b>hours, days, weeks or months</b>. Comorbid with <b>major depressive disorder, anxiety disorders</b> and the <b>avoidant, borderline and obsessive-compulsive personality disorders</b>. Risk factors: <b>certain personality traits, serious trauma as a child or adult, severe stress, depression and anxiety</b>, and <b>substance misuse</b>. <b>Psychotherapy is the main treatment</b>, either <b>CBT or psychodynamic therapy</b>, with <b>self-hypnosis and rTMS</b> also used."},{"h":"<b>Describe the symptoms and treatment of adjustment disorder.</b> An emotional or behavioral reaction <b>within 3 months of a stressor</b>, <b>out of proportion to its severity</b>, that affects the ability to function and <b>ends by 6 months</b>. Milder than PTSD or acute stress disorder. Symptoms are <b>anxiety, depression or mixed</b>, and in children regressive, fearful or acting-out behavior. <b>Building coping skills is the key intervention.</b>"}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":[],"refs":["hyper-hypoarousal"]},{"id":"anxiety","title":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","chapters":"Chapter 15","questions":5,"week":5,"isNew":false,"meds":"out","focus":["Clinical features and evidence-based therapies for separation anxiety disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, social anxiety disorder, panic disorder and specific phobias","The same for the OCD-related disorders: obsessive-compulsive disorder and body dysmorphic disorder","Telling the disorders apart, therapeutic communication and interventions","The Clinical Judgment Model applied to anxiety and OCD-related disorders"],"out":["Hoarding disorder, trichotillomania and excoriation disorder"],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","section":"anxiety-ocd","page":"week5-anxiety-ocd-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders","terms":["1g0pnag","197nct0","y50qqe","nnf5yd","11kjzus","qns3o7","1gskqbh","khfoy8","snnmv4","12mttpn","1x26sul","1ao2d7t","18y2kn7","1fus9v8","1bgjzzq","w7bahi","thttro","9puc4r","e25sao"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of GAD and separation anxiety disorder.</b> <b>GAD</b>: excessive worry <b>more days than not for at least 6 months</b>, with <b>at least 3 of 6 symptoms, or 1 in children</b>: restlessness, easy fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, sleep disturbance. Often presents in <b>primary care with somatic complaints</b>. <b>Separation anxiety</b> is normal from <b>8 months, peaking at 18 months</b>. The disorder is developmentally inappropriate concern over separation, and in adults appears as harm avoidance, worry and shyness."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of the phobias, social anxiety and panic disorder.</b> <b>Specific phobia</b>: irrational fear of an object or situation, <b>always anticipated</b> and avoided. <b>Agoraphobia</b>: fear of places where <b>escape is difficult or help unavailable</b>, such as crowds, bridges and elevators. <b>Social anxiety</b>: fear of <b>negative evaluation</b>, with <b>public speaking the most common</b>, plus blushing and sweating. <b>Panic disorder</b>: sudden attacks lasting minutes with chest pain, palpitations, choking and fear of dying, plus <b>anticipatory anxiety</b> and <b>avoidance anxiety</b>."},{"h":"<b>Describe the clinical manifestations of OCD and body dysmorphic disorder.</b> <b>OCD</b>: obsessions and compulsions that are <b>time-consuming</b> and distressing, with <b>shame</b>, gradual onset and <b>worsening under stress</b>. <b>Body dysmorphic disorder</b>: preoccupation with unseen flaws, mirror checking and camouflaging, with <b>high suicide risk</b>."},{"h":"<b>Discuss the psychological treatments for anxiety disorders.</b> <b>CBT</b> identifies and changes faulty thinking, and is <b>first line for panic disorder</b>. <b>Systematic desensitization</b> is gradual exposure with relaxation at each step. <b>Flooding</b> is prolonged exposure to a large amount of the stimulus. <b>Exposure and response prevention</b> treats OCD, and <b>CBT is for adolescents only</b> in children with OCD. <b>DBS</b> is approved for OCD at <b>18 and older</b>, and <b>TMS for adults 22 to 68</b>. Relaxation methods include deep breathing, progressive muscle relaxation and biofeedback, practiced daily."}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":["anxiety-levels","interventions-by-level"],"refs":[]},{"id":"schizophrenia","title":"Schizophrenia","chapters":"Chapter 12","questions":5,"week":4,"isNew":false,"meds":"in","focus":["Telling the positive, negative, cognitive and affective symptoms apart","Care considerations: priority problems, outcomes and goals, interventions, evaluation"],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","section":"schizophrenia","page":"week4-schizophrenia.html","pageTitle":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder","terms":["9hxqrp","1bfh2pg","1gv53f1","149n9xt","jledh4","ki62qq","jxn1s","s83fcv","a6er5v","rewq3u","a3i8rv","vlivhx","e72l6n","19f1zvd","g2zk5v","36371u","tiv2aa","19jhxvf","4sqczc","10rl4yn","1488tkp","y9u5cx","1p8pdpi","5fckzb","dq9tot","16xx1a3"],"bullets":[{"s":1,"h":"<b>Know which symptoms each classification treats.</b> <b>Typical antipsychotics primarily treat the positive symptoms and have little effect on the negative symptoms.</b> <b>Atypical antipsychotics treat both positive and negative symptoms.</b>"},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Know the medications that fall under each classification, and the side effects and adverse reactions typical of that classification.</b> <b>Typical</b>: chlorpromazine, haloperidol, and haloperidol decanoate as a long-acting injection. <b>Atypical</b>: clozapine, olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone, aripiprazole, with risperidone and paliperidone palmitate as long-acting injections. <b>Anticholinergics used to treat side effects</b>: benztropine, trihexyphenidyl."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Know how to treat or manage the medication side effects and adverse reactions.</b> <b>Acute dystonia</b>: monitor and act emergently if the airway is involved. <b>Akathisia</b>: reduce the dose or change medication, add benztropine, and propranolol, lorazepam or diazepam short-term only. <b>Pseudoparkinsonism</b>: identify and slowly discontinue the drug, reduce the dose, or add benztropine or trihexyphenidyl. <b>Tardive dyskinesia</b>: discontinue or reduce, switch to an atypical by tapering one down while tapering the other up, and valbenazine or deutetrabenazine. <b>NMS</b>: stop all antipsychotics immediately, ICU, dantrolene sodium and bromocriptine mesylate, lorazepam for agitation, cooling and hydration."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Agranulocytosis is a side effect of taking clozapine.</b> A reduction in circulating granulocytes that limits the ability to fight infection. Life-threatening, greatest risk in the first months, <b>monitor WBC weekly for the first 18 weeks</b>, and reversible if treated early."},{"h":"<b>Differentiate the positive symptoms</b>, which <b>exist but should not be there</b>: alterations in <b>perception</b> (paranoia, delusions, hallucinations), <b>speech</b>, <b>thought</b> and <b>behavior</b>."},{"h":"<b>Differentiate the negative symptoms</b>, which <b>should be there but are not</b>: affective blunting, alogia, anhedonia, apathy, asociality and avolition. They are a serious problem because they amount to the <b>absence of essential human qualities</b>."},{"h":"<b>Differentiate the cognitive and affective symptoms.</b> <b>Cognitive</b>: anosognosia, concrete thinking, impaired memory, impaired information processing and impaired executive functioning. <b>Affective</b>: an altered experience and expression of emotion, with mood that is unstable, erratic, labile or incongruent."},{"h":"<b>Discuss care considerations.</b> <b>Priority problems</b> are led by <b>safety</b>: risk for violence toward others, risk for suicide or self-harm, disturbed thought processes and sensory perception, risk for non-adherence. <b>Outcomes</b> are specific and measurable with a timeframe, developed with the patient. <b>Interventions</b>: assess with the C-SSRS each shift, assess orientation, teach one coping strategy each shift, administer medications and PRNs for agitation, and educate patient and family. <b>Evaluate</b> against safety, physical care, medication stabilization, education and psychosocial support."}]}],"roster":[["chlorpromazine<br> haloperidol","Typical antipsychotic","Schizophrenia. Treat positive symptoms, little effect on negative."],["haloperidol decanoate","Typical antipsychotic, long-acting injection","Schizophrenia where non-adherence is a risk. Given every 2 to 4 weeks or longer."],["olanzapine<br> quetiapine<br> aripiprazole","Atypical antipsychotic","Schizophrenia. Treat both positive and negative symptoms."],["risperidone","Atypical antipsychotic, also a long-acting injection","Schizophrenia, including in children and adolescents."],["paliperidone palmitate","Atypical antipsychotic, long-acting injection","Schizophrenia where non-adherence is a risk."],["clozapine","Atypical antipsychotic","Treatment-resistant schizophrenia, after two failed monotherapy trials. Causes agranulocytosis."],["benztropine<br> trihexyphenidyl","Anticholinergic","Treat extrapyramidal side effects: akathisia and pseudoparkinsonism."],["propranolol","Beta blocker","Added for akathisia."],["lorazepam<br> diazepam","Benzodiazepine","Akathisia, short-term only. Lorazepam also for agitation in NMS."],["valbenazine<br> deutetrabenazine","VMAT2 inhibitor","Manage the symptoms of tardive dyskinesia."],["dantrolene sodium<br> bromocriptine mesylate","NMS treatment","Relieve muscle rigidity and reduce fever in neuroleptic malignant syndrome."]],"rosterHref":"medications.html#w4-glance","medSections":[{"id":"w4-typical","title":"Typical Antipsychotics","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>chlorpromazine</b></li>\n      <li><b>haloperidol</b></li>\n      <li><b>haloperidol decanoate</b>, the long-acting injectable form</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder.</b> <b>Primarily treat the positive symptoms, with little effect on the negative symptoms.</b> Used less often than atypicals for that reason, but <b>as effective as newer agents for positive symptoms and much less expensive</b>. <b>2 to 6 weeks to become effective.</b></td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>Extrapyramidal side effects</b> are the most common and distressing problem, and the leading driver of non-adherence. Also <b>anticholinergic effects, sedation, weight gain, sexual dysfunction, endocrine disturbance</b> and <b>orthostatic hypotension</b>. Treat EPS with a <b>dose reduction or medication change</b>, and with an <b>anticholinergic agent such as benztropine or trihexyphenidyl</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</b> · <b>tardive dyskinesia</b> · <b>metabolic syndrome</b> · <b>cardiac arrhythmias</b> · an <b>increased risk of seizures</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>Assess with the AIMS at least every 3 months</b> for tardive dyskinesia. <b>Monotherapy is recommended</b>, with patient-specific dose adjustment. Teach that the class is <b>unlikely to be lethal in overdose and is not addictive</b>, and that <b>stopping the medication almost certainly leads to relapse</b>. <b>Short-acting injectable forms intensify side effects</b> and are harder to manage.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"},{"id":"w4-atypical","title":"Atypical Antipsychotics","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>clozapine</b></li>\n      <li><b>olanzapine</b></li>\n      <li><b>quetiapine</b></li>\n      <li><b>risperidone</b>, also a long-acting injectable</li>\n      <li><b>aripiprazole</b></li>\n      <li><b>paliperidone palmitate</b>, a long-acting injectable</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Schizophrenia and schizoaffective disorder.</b> <b>Treat both positive and negative symptoms</b>, which is why they are used more often than typicals. <b>Risperidone and aripiprazole</b> are the agents used in <b>children and adolescents</b>. <b>2 to 6 weeks to become effective.</b></td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>Sedation</b> and <b>sexual dysfunction</b>. <b>Less likely than typicals to cause anticholinergic effects, orthostatic hypotension, seizures and EPS</b>, and side effects overall are <b>fewer, milder and better tolerated</b>. They are <b>more costly</b>, which can itself become a barrier to adherence.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Metabolic syndrome</b>, the signature risk of the class: <b>significant weight gain, especially abdominal, dyslipidemia, increased blood glucose and insulin resistance</b>, raising the risk of <b>diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular disease and certain cancers</b>. Also <b>hyperprolactinemia</b>, <b>cardiac dysrhythmias</b> and <b>sudden cardiac death</b>. <b>Less likely to cause tardive dyskinesia or significant EPS</b>, but not exempt.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td>For metabolic syndrome: <b>monitor weight and girth</b>, order an <b>initial glucose tolerance test</b>, <b>monitor blood glucose regularly</b>, and <b>provide nutrition and activity support</b> while considering the patient's lifestyle. <b>Assess with the AIMS at least every 3 months.</b> <b>Long-acting injectables are given every 2 to 4 weeks or up to months</b> and require <b>transportation to the clinic</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-danger\"><b>All atypical antipsychotics carry a risk of metabolic syndrome.</b> It is the reason weight, girth and blood glucose are monitored from the start of therapy rather than after a problem appears.</div>"},{"id":"w4-clozapine","title":"Clozapine","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>clozapine</b></li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Treatment-resistant or refractory schizophrenia</b>, and schizoaffective disorder, where the patient has had an <b>inadequate response to two other antipsychotics</b> or could not tolerate them. <b>A clozapine trial is justified after the failure of two monotherapy trials.</b> Refractory patients are about <b>30%</b> of the thought disorder population and are <b>prone to violence and suicide</b>. Use has produced <b>decreased negative symptoms, increased impulse control, reduced violence toward self and others</b>, and improved quality of life.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td>The atypical class effects above, including <b>metabolic syndrome</b> and <b>sedation</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Agranulocytosis</b>, a <b>potentially fatal</b> reduction in circulating granulocytes that <b>limits the ability to fight off infection</b>. <b>Greatest risk during the first months of treatment.</b> <b>Reversible if treated early.</b></td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>Monitor WBC weekly for the first 18 weeks of treatment</b>, then as directed by the provider. <b>Discontinue if the patient develops leucopenia or neutropenia.</b> Teach the patient to <b>report any sign of infection</b>, since a falling white count presents as infection rather than as a symptom of its own.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-danger\"><b>Agranulocytosis is the reason clozapine is not first-line.</b> It is the one fact to carry about this drug: a life-threatening drop in white blood cells, monitored weekly for the first 18 weeks.</div>"},{"id":"w4-anticholinergics","title":"Anticholinergics for Extrapyramidal Side Effects","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>benztropine</b></li>\n      <li><b>trihexyphenidyl</b></li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Treating the extrapyramidal side effects of antipsychotic medication.</b> <b>Benztropine</b> is given for <b>akathisia</b> and <b>pseudoparkinsonism</b>. <b>Trihexyphenidyl</b> is given for <b>pseudoparkinsonism</b>. They treat the side effect, not the schizophrenia.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>Anticholinergic effects</b>, the thing the drug itself produces: <b>dry mouth, blurred vision, dry eyes, constipation, urinary retention or hesitancy, drowsiness, dizziness, confusion, tachycardia</b> and <b>skin flushing from decreased sweating</b>. Manage with fluids, fiber and eye drops, and watch for urinary retention.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Anticholinergic toxicity</b>, which the side effects can progress to.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td>Teach the mnemonic pattern so the patient can recognise the effects: <b>mad as a hatter, blind as a bat, red as a beet, hot as a hare, dry as a bone</b>. Adding an anticholinergic to an antipsychotic <b>compounds the anticholinergic burden</b>, so the combined effect is assessed, not just the new drug's.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"},{"id":"w4-adverse-treatment","title":"Treating Akathisia, Tardive Dyskinesia & NMS","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>propranolol</b> · <b>lorazepam</b> · <b>diazepam</b>, for akathisia</li>\n      <li><b>valbenazine</b> · <b>deutetrabenazine</b>, for tardive dyskinesia</li>\n      <li><b>dantrolene sodium</b> · <b>bromocriptine mesylate</b> · <b>lorazepam</b>, for NMS</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Akathisia</b>: <b>propranolol</b>, or <b>lorazepam or diazepam</b>, added by the provider alongside a dose reduction and benztropine. <b>Tardive dyskinesia</b>: <b>valbenazine</b> and <b>deutetrabenazine</b>, the two FDA-approved agents for managing symptoms. <b>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</b>: <b>dantrolene sodium</b> and <b>bromocriptine mesylate</b> to relieve muscle rigidity and reduce fever, with <b>lorazepam</b> for agitation.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Side Effects &amp; What to Do</td><td><b>Benzodiazepines are for short-term use only</b> in akathisia. Longer use risks dependence and adds sedation to a patient already at risk of it.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>Akathisia is easily mistaken for anxiety or agitation</b>, and treating it as such means giving more of the antipsychotic that caused it, making it worse. <b>Assess before medicating.</b> In <b>tardive dyskinesia</b>, symptoms usually <b>persist even after the antipsychotic dose is lowered or stopped</b>, so these drugs manage rather than reverse it. In <b>NMS</b>, the first action is always to <b>stop all antipsychotics</b>, with ICU care, hydration, cooling and vital sign monitoring.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-danger\"><b>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a medical emergency with a 10% fatality rate.</b> <b>Stop all antipsychotics immediately.</b> Severe muscle rigidity, altered mental status, temperature over 103 degrees Fahrenheit, hypertension, tachycardia, tachypnea, diaphoresis and incontinence. ICU care is required, and an accurate medication history is critical because it is difficult to diagnose emergently.</div>"}],"lineups":["delusions-do-do-not"],"refs":[]},{"id":"sud","title":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","chapters":"Chapter 22","questions":5,"week":3,"isNew":false,"meds":"in","focus":["Clinical manifestations of use, addiction, intoxication, withdrawal, tolerance and overdose, and treatment","Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff syndrome: symptoms, progression, management","Psychological treatments for managing withdrawal and promoting recovery","Use of the CIWA and the COWS","The clinical judgment model applied to substance use disorders"],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","section":"sud","page":"week3-substance-use-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders","terms":["1vhi2l9","sp42of","1fs9ahy","1suvgrc","ji2evs","1qdbs6m","1a3h5x","ri77sg","7ju9dh","10oh7vp","jygcye"],"bullets":[{"s":1,"h":"<b>Differentiate the signs and symptoms of the two main withdrawal syndromes, and know how to treat each.</b> <b>Opioid withdrawal</b>: tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, enlarged pupils, lacrimation, rhinorrhea, piloerection, muscle spasms, bone and joint aches, GI cramping and diarrhea, yawning, insomnia. <b>Alcohol or benzodiazepine (sedative-hypnotic) withdrawal</b>: tremors, anxiety, agitation, nausea, rising vital signs, then hallucinosis, then seizures, then delirium tremens. They look very different from each other."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Know the opioid withdrawal symptoms on the COWS:</b> resting pulse rate, sweating, restlessness, pupil size, bone or joint aches, runny nose or tearing, GI upset, tremor, yawning, anxiety or irritability, gooseflesh skin."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Explain the symptoms, progression and management of Wernicke's encephalopathy and Korsakoff syndrome</b>: both stages, caused by thiamine (B1) deficiency, the first reversible and the second not."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Know these eleven medications:</b> acamprosate, diazepam, disulfiram, lorazepam, thiamine, clonidine, naloxone, buprenorphine, buprenorphine &amp; naloxone, methadone, naltrexone."},{"h":"<b>Discuss clinical manifestations of use, intoxication, withdrawal, tolerance, overdose and treatment.</b> Opioid overdose is an emergency. The cardinal signs are coma, pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression. Give naloxone and repeat if signs return, because its half-life is shorter than the opioid's."},{"h":"<b>Identify pharmacologic and psychological treatments for withdrawal and recovery.</b> Alcohol withdrawal: diazepam or lorazepam at a CIWA-Ar of 8–10 or more. Recovery: naltrexone, disulfiram and acamprosate, none of which treats withdrawal. Opioids: methadone, buprenorphine, buprenorphine &amp; naloxone, naltrexone, clonidine. Psychological: individual and behavioral therapy, CBT, family therapy, social skills training, support groups."},{"h":"<b>Discuss the use of the CIWA and the COWS.</b> COWS assesses opioid withdrawal. CIWA assesses sedative-hypnotic and alcohol withdrawal, scoring nausea/vomiting, tremors, anxiety, agitation, paroxysmal sweats, headache, tactile, auditory and visual disturbance, and orientation, usually every 4 hours and PRN."},{"h":"<b>Apply the clinical judgment measurement model</b> to individuals with substance use and addictive disorders: recognize and analyze cues through screening and assessment, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take action, evaluate outcomes. Involving relatives in treatment decreases relapse rates by 20 to 50 percent."}]}],"roster":[["naloxone","Opioid antagonist","Reverses opioid overdose."],["methadone","Synthetic narcotic opioid","Opioid withdrawal and maintenance."],["buprenorphine","Partial opioid agonist","Reduces opioid cravings and withdrawal symptoms."],["buprenorphine &amp; naloxone","Combination","Reduce or quit opioids. Relieves cravings and reduces mortality."],["naltrexone","Opioid antagonist","Recovery in both opioid and alcohol use disorder: blocks the high, reduces cravings."],["clonidine","Antihypertensive, alpha agonist","Withdrawal symptoms, especially HR and BP in opioid, alcohol or nicotine withdrawal. Elevated BP in DTs."],["diazepam","Benzodiazepine","Alcohol withdrawal, DTs, withdrawal seizures."],["lorazepam","Benzodiazepine","Alcohol withdrawal. IV if delirium appears."],["disulfiram","Aversive agent","Deters drinking by causing severe illness if alcohol is taken."],["acamprosate","Anti-craving agent","Reduces alcohol cravings, maintains abstinence."],["thiamine","Vitamin B1","Replaced daily in alcohol use disorder. Treats Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome."]],"rosterHref":"medications.html#w3-glance","medSections":[{"id":"w3-opioid-overdose","title":"Opioid Overdose Reversal","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>naloxone</b></li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Reverses an opioid overdose</b>: an opioid antagonist that displaces the opioid from its receptor. Also used with buprenorphine as a combination product for opioid use disorder. <b>Do not confuse it with naltrexone</b>, the recovery-phase antagonist that reduces cravings.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Sends the patient into instant withdrawal</b> by throwing the opioid off the receptor.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td>Available as a <b>nasal spray or injection</b>: intranasal, IM, sub-q or IV. <b>Its half-life is shorter than that of opioids, so keep watching and repeat the dose if signs return.</b> <b>Naloxone kits go home</b> with patients at risk of overdose, and family or friends are taught the signs of severe intoxication and impending overdose.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-danger\"><b>Opioid overdose is an emergency.</b> Outside hospital, <b>call 9-1-1 and give naloxone if available</b>. In hospital, <b>promote breathing first</b>: aspirate secretions, insert an airway, ventilate. Then give naloxone. The cardinal signs are <b>coma, pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression</b>.</div>"},{"id":"w3-oud","title":"Medication-Assisted Treatment: Opioid Use Disorder","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Opioid agonists</b>: methadone · buprenorphine · buprenorphine &amp; naloxone</li>\n      <li><b>Antagonist</b>: naltrexone</li>\n      <li><b>Alpha agonist</b>: clonidine</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Methadone</b>: the first medication marketed for opioid withdrawal. Acts like heroin, morphine and fentanyl but far more slowly, flattening the highs and the withdrawal lows. <b>Buprenorphine</b>: long-acting and slow, reducing cravings and withdrawal so the patient can focus on recovery. <b>Buprenorphine &amp; naloxone</b>: produces a weaker euphoria than heroin or methadone, relieves cravings and withdrawal symptoms, and <b>reduces the mortality of opioid use disorder by 50%</b> by reducing the risk of overdose. <b>Naltrexone</b>: blocks the effects of opioids and alcohol, reducing cravings and preventing relapse. <b>Clonidine</b>: relieves withdrawal symptoms, especially <b>heart rate and blood pressure</b>, and the anxiety and agitation common in withdrawal from <b>alcohol, opioids or nicotine</b>. Used alongside drugs like methadone.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Methadone carries significant overdose potential and dependence is still possible.</b> Buprenorphine does not carry methadone's overdose potential.</td></tr>\n      <!-- SETTLED 2026-09-11 on Holly's explicit call: 10 TO 14 DAYS. The\n           recording said 7 to 10; the instructor's \"Week 3 - MAT\n           TABLE-2.docx\" says 10 to 14, and Holly chose the MAT figure for\n           the whole site (the topic page's callout-danger too). Do not\n           revert to 7 to 10. -->\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Contraindications</td><td><b>Naltrexone requires 10 to 14 days opioid-free before the first dose</b>. Sooner precipitates a rapid, severe withdrawal.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>Methadone must be monitored closely</b>, with <b>daily clinic visits</b>. For replacement therapy, stabilize the patient first, then <b>taper slowly until off methadone</b> to prevent more severe withdrawal. <b>Naltrexone does not treat withdrawal symptoms.</b> Clonidine works by blocking the neurotransmitters that trigger sympathetic activity.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"two-col\">\n    <div class=\"card\">\n      <h4>Buprenorphine Alone</h4>\n      <ul>\n        <li><b>Preferred for induction</b>: starting treatment under the supervision of a healthcare provider.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"card alt\">\n      <h4>Buprenorphine &amp; Naloxone</h4>\n      <ul>\n        <li>The usual switch <b>after initial treatment</b>, once the medication is <b>no longer taken under direct supervision</b>.</li>\n        <li>Naloxone is a <b>safety feature</b> that discourages dissolving and injecting the medication.</li>\n        <li>Naloxone is absorbed <b>only if the medication is injected</b> instead of dissolved in the mouth as directed, and then it causes uncomfortable withdrawal symptoms.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<p class=\"note\"><b>In pregnancy:</b> maintenance decreases dose variability and limits exposure to dirty needles and unknown additives. Dosage may be <b>titrated upward as pregnancy progresses</b>, with <b>twice-daily dosing</b> as an option, titrated against opioid withdrawal scoring, and maintained until delivery. <b>The fetus experiences withdrawal 2–3 times as intense as the mother's, and can die from it.</b> Buprenorphine/naloxone has largely replaced methadone as the standard.</p>"},{"id":"w3-alcohol-withdrawal","title":"Benzodiazepines for Alcohol Withdrawal","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>Benzodiazepines</b>: diazepam · lorazepam</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p><b>In DTs:</b> seizures → IV diazepam · acute agitation, tremors, impending or acute DTs, hallucinosis → oral diazepam · delirium appears → IV lorazepam · elevated BP → clonidine · dehydration → oral or IV fluids.</p>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Alcohol and sedative-hypnotic withdrawal</b>, given <b>at a CIWA-Ar score of 8 to 10 or more</b>. Goals: <b>control agitation, decrease the risk of seizures, and reduce morbidity and mortality</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td>The patient may be on a <b>fixed, scheduled regimen with PRN doses</b> for breakthrough autonomic symptoms: for example <b>diazepam 10 mg every 6 hours for four doses, then 5 mg every 6 hours for eight doses</b>. <b>Diazepam is the current standard at UK HealthCare</b> because its <b>long half-life</b> works better and it is particularly effective at decreasing seizures. Rescore with the CIWA whenever the patient reports worsening symptoms.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>\n<div class=\"callout-danger\"><b>Delirium tremens is the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal and can result in death.</b> Prevention is the goal: assess and monitor symptoms, labs, electrolytes and vital signs, hydrate, medicate adequately, and provide life-sustaining measures if indicated.</div>"},{"id":"w3-aud","title":"Medication-Assisted Treatment: Alcohol Use Disorder","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>naltrexone</b>: also used in opioid use disorder</li>\n      <li><b>disulfiram</b></li>\n      <li><b>acamprosate</b></li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>A combination of medication with individual and behavioral therapies is what sustains recovery.</p>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Naltrexone</b>: blocks the euphoric effects of opioids and alcohol, so relapse produces no high, and reduces alcohol cravings. Long-acting, given <b>IM</b>. <b>Disulfiram</b>: <b>prevents the breakdown of acetaldehyde</b>, so drinking while taking it produces severely unpleasant flu-like illness. It <b>does not reduce cravings</b>. <b>Acamprosate</b>: reduces cravings and helps maintain abstinence.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Adverse Events</td><td><b>Disulfiram plus any alcohol produces severe flu-like illness.</b></td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>None of the three treats withdrawal symptoms. All are recovery-phase medications.</b> Disulfiram teaching: <b>alcohol is not only in drinks</b>. Patients must avoid being near, touching or consuming alcohol-based products — <b>perfumes, aftershaves, toners, mouthwash, many liquid medications</b> (especially nighttime formulas) and <b>paint thinners</b>. Even splashing on aftershave can make them severely ill. Alongside these, treat withdrawal symptoms symptomatically and <b>replace thiamine daily</b>.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"},{"id":"w3-thiamine","title":"Thiamine & Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome","html":"<div class=\"card\">\n    <h4>Drugs</h4>\n    <ul>\n      <li><b>thiamine</b>: vitamin B1</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Indication</td><td><b>Replaced daily in alcohol use disorder</b>: the standard regimen is a multivitamin, thiamine and folic acid. Treats <b>Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome</b>, a neurological disorder caused by lack of thiamine (B1). Chronic alcohol use <b>decreases intestinal absorption of thiamine by about 70%</b>.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td class=\"term\">Monitoring &amp; Teaching</td><td><b>Wernicke's encephalopathy</b>: large doses, <b>IV, 2–3 times daily for 1–2 weeks</b>, typically about 10 days. Acute and <b>reversible</b>. May clear in a few weeks or progress. <b>Korsakoff syndrome</b>: thiamine replacement for <b>3–12 months</b>, varying with symptoms, severity and whether psychosis is present. Chronic and not typically reversible, with a <b>recovery rate of only about 20%</b>. Rule out other causes of thiamine deficiency: eating disorders, bariatric surgery, poor nutrition, chronic infection, bowel surgery.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table></div>"}],"lineups":["wernicke-korsakoff","opioid-intoxication-overdose"],"refs":[]},{"id":"bipolar","title":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","chapters":"Chapter 13","questions":5,"week":2,"isNew":false,"meds":"out","focus":["Bipolar I","Bipolar II","Mania","Hypomania","Treatment other than medications","Nursing care"],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","section":"bipolar","page":"week2-bipolar-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Bipolar & Related Disorders","terms":["bqztsm","smbqzc"],"bullets":[{"s":1,"h":"<b>Signs and symptoms of bipolar I and bipolar II.</b> Bipolar I: at least one manic episode, the most severe form, highest mortality. Bipolar II: at least one hypomanic plus at least one major depressive episode, never a manic one."},{"h":"<b>Distinguish a manic from a hypomanic episode:</b> 1 week versus at least 4 consecutive days, marked impairment or hospitalization versus neither, psychotic features possible versus none."},{"h":"<b>Nursing care for an individual with a bipolar disorder.</b> Through the first days of acute mania the focus is degree of <b>exhaustion and nutrition</b>. The primary outcome is injury prevention."}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":["manic-hypomanic"],"refs":[]},{"id":"depressive","title":"Depressive Disorders","chapters":"Chapter 14","questions":5,"week":2,"isNew":false,"meds":"out","focus":["Major depressive disorder","Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder","Persistent depressive disorder (formerly dysthymia)","Symptoms, and treatments other than medications","The nursing process and clinical judgment model applied to depressive disorders"],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Depressive Disorders","section":"depressive","page":"week2-depressive-disorders.html","pageTitle":"Depressive Disorders","terms":["u6ygdl","19l5ee2","1bu7snl","xjt1f6"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>The four depressive disorders, and how to tell them apart:</b> major depressive disorder, disruptive mood dysregulation disorder, premenstrual dysphoric disorder, persistent depressive disorder. Know the symptoms, treatments, and nursing care of each."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Symptoms of depression to recognize:</b> hopelessness, sadness, worthlessness, shame, anhedonia, anxiety, self-harming behavior, and feeling unable to keep oneself safe."},{"s":1,"h":"<b>Apply the nursing process and clinical judgment model</b> to the care of individuals with depressive disorders."}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":[],"refs":[]},{"id":"anger","title":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","chapters":"Chapter 27","questions":4,"week":1,"isNew":false,"meds":"none","focus":[],"out":[],"notes":[],"topics":[{"label":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","section":"anger","page":"week1-anger-aggression-violence.html","pageTitle":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","terms":["er5dn","9dpcrc","xy4gct","otgt58","921glv","13b3m8c"],"bullets":[{"h":"<b>Four predictors of violence:</b> history of violence, recent acts of violence, using profanity, hyperactivity."},{"h":"<b>Of those four:</b> hyperactivity is the most important predictor of <i>imminent</i> violence. A history of violence is the single best predictor of <i>future</i> violence."},{"h":"<b>Precipitants:</b> anxiety (catch it early), withdrawal and substance use, sleep deprivation, pain, feeling out of control, and poor communication."},{"h":"<b>Warning signs:</b> pacing and restlessness, clenched jaws or fists, changes in speech rate or volume, verbal abuse, glaring or wholly avoided eye contact, and uncharacteristic withdrawal."},{"h":"<b>Aggression occurs most often in the context of limit setting.</b>"},{"h":"<b>The four levels:</b> anxiety → loss of rationality (defensive) → hostility, abuse or aggression → tension reduction, the reachable and teachable moment."},{"h":"<b>De-escalation:</b> speak slowly in shortened sentences with a low, calm voice. Offer two options to reduce powerlessness. Give feedback on what you see. Never yell."},{"h":"<b>Nurse safety:</b> stay about one foot further than the patient can reach. Keep an escape route and never let the patient between you and the door. No dangling jewellery. Only one person talks."},{"h":"<b>Restraint hard rules:</b> never prone, never a PRN or standing order, order and face-to-face assessment within one hour, two fingers under the restraint."},{"h":"<b>What <i>silently</i> counts as a restraint:</b> four side rails up (except seizure precautions), sheets tucked so tightly the patient cannot move, holding someone against their will."},{"h":"<b>Seclusion is tried before restraint.</b> Both are a last resort, only when the patient is a danger to self or others and less restrictive interventions have been found ineffective."},{"h":"<b>A restrained patient is never left alone.</b> Remove restraints and do range of motion on one limb at a time, and always place a wedge pillow under the head of anyone restrained in bed."},{"h":"<b>Six contraindications to seclusion and restraint:</b> extremely unstable medical or psychiatric conditions, COPD, spinal injury, seizure disorders, pregnancy, delirium or dementia."}]}],"roster":null,"rosterHref":"","medSections":[],"lineups":[],"refs":[]}],"offFinal":["Introduction to PMH Nursing","Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Assessment, Theories & Therapies","Group & Physiologic Therapies","Therapeutic Communication","Suicide","Nonsuicidal Self-Injury","Personality Disorders","Eating & Feeding Disorders","Caring for Persons with Abuse"],"cards":[{"id":"mt4dhk","t":"Resiliency","d":"<b>Bouncing back from adversity</b>, and a <b>protective factor against mental illness</b>. A resilient child adapts to change, relies on inner strength and healthy coping, functions amid strong emotions, reaches out for help, and <b>forms nurturing relationships with other adults when a parent is unavailable</b>.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Childhood Mental Health"},{"id":"1ohhwey","t":"Play therapy","d":"The <b>expression of feelings through play</b>, the \"language\" of children. For <b>younger children</b>, <b>rooted in psychodynamic therapy</b>, and <b>cannot be standardized</b>. The therapist is a <b>trusted participant, never the aberrant perpetrator</b>.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Childhood Mental Health"},{"id":"be3yxh","t":"Tic disorders","d":"<b>Sudden, nonrhythmic, rapid motor movements or vocalizations.</b> Three types: <b>Tourette's disorder</b>, the most severe, with multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics for more than a year · <b>persistent motor or vocal tic disorder</b>, more than 1 year · <b>provisional tic disorder</b>, less than 1 year.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders"},{"id":"915oky","t":"Intellectual developmental disorder","d":"<b>Deficits in intellectual, social and daily functioning.</b> Cognitive and social stimulation can raise functioning <b>if begun before age 5</b>.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders"},{"id":"yfzuuk","t":"Autism spectrum disorder","d":"<b>Deficits in social interactions and relationships</b>, <b>stereotypical repetitive speech or behavior</b>, <b>obsessive focus on specific objects</b>, <b>over-adherence to routines</b> and <b>extreme resistance to change</b>, and <b>hyper- or hypo-reactivity to sensory input</b>. Appears in early childhood.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders"},{"id":"150vp65","t":"ADHD","d":"A <b>persistent pattern of inattention, hyperactivity and impulsiveness</b>, inappropriate for developmental level, <b>in at least 2 settings</b>, causing difficulties <b>for at least 6 months, before age 12</b>.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders"},{"id":"10xyfxe","t":"Oppositional defiant disorder","d":"A pattern of <b>angry or irritable mood, argumentative or defiant behavior, or vindictiveness</b> lasting <b>at least 6 months</b>. Usually diagnosed <b>around age 8</b>. May become conduct disorder, though <b>most children outgrow it</b>.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Impulse Control Disorders"},{"id":"1fp117g","t":"Conduct disorder","d":"<b>Repetitive, persistent patterns of behavior that violate the basic rights of others</b> or major age-appropriate norms or rules. <b>Lacks empathy and guilt.</b> Guarded prognosis, and <b>may be a precursor to antisocial personality disorder</b>.","w":6,"r":"childhood","g":"Impulse Control Disorders"},{"id":"1wr8hyc","t":"Cognition","d":"System of interrelated abilities such as <b>perception, reasoning, judgment, intuition and memory</b>. Allows one to be aware of oneself in relation to others.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"1d2n3in","t":"Memory","d":"Facet of cognition: <b>retaining and recalling past experiences</b>.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"15m74dj","t":"Delirium","d":"<b>Acute</b> cognitive impairment with <b>rapid onset</b>, caused by a <b>medical condition</b>.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"e7wh0l","t":"Dementia","d":"<b>Chronic</b> cognitive impairment, <b>differentiated by cause, not symptoms</b>.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"wm0ndi","t":"Sundowning","d":"Tendency for <b>mood to deteriorate and agitation to increase in the later part of the day</b>, with the fading of light, or at night.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"7y2q31","t":"Aphasia","d":"<b>Loss of language ability.</b> <b>Expressive</b>: cannot find the words to express ideas (Broca's area). <b>Receptive</b>: cannot interpret what is said (Wernicke's).","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"2s16e2","t":"Apraxia","d":"<b>Loss of purposeful movement.</b>","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"81o59u","t":"Agnosia","d":"<b>Loss of ability to recognize objects.</b>","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"1c1r1qt","t":"Confabulation","d":"<b>Unconscious creation of stories or answers in place of actual memories.</b> Maintains self-esteem.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"10aopmt","t":"Perseveration","d":"<b>Persistent repetition of a word, phrase or gesture.</b>","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"3i6li6","t":"Hyperorality","d":"<b>Tendency to put things in the mouth</b> to taste and chew.","w":7,"r":"neurocognitive","g":"Neurocognitive Disorders"},{"id":"1f8cf7i","t":"La belle indifference","d":"A <b>lack of concern or emotional response</b> despite having a serious medical illness or physical symptoms. Most often associated with <b>conversion disorder</b>.","w":6,"r":"somatic","g":"Somatic Symptom Disorders"},{"id":"1rjfhp0","t":"Malingering","d":"<b>Intentionally exaggerating or fabricating</b> physical or psychological symptoms <b>for external gain</b>, such as avoiding work, admission to a hospital or psychiatric facility, financial compensation or prescription medications.","w":6,"r":"somatic","g":"Somatic Symptom Disorders"},{"id":"5wbwg0","t":"Primary gain","d":"A benefit that <b>protects from internal anxiety</b>, emotional symptoms or conflicts. Produces <b>internal motivators</b>.","w":6,"r":"somatic","g":"Somatic Symptom Disorders"},{"id":"1k3oaww","t":"Secondary gain","d":"<b>Relieves the individual from external expected responsibilities</b>: more attention and sympathy, missing work, hospital admission, financial assistance, disability compensation, prescription medications, avoiding a jail sentence. Produces <b>external motivators</b>.","w":6,"r":"somatic","g":"Somatic Symptom Disorders"},{"id":"czqi20","t":"Medical mimics","d":"Disorders that present with <b>psychiatric symptoms directly resulting from an underlying medical condition</b>. The cause of the psychiatric symptoms is the medical illness.","w":7,"r":"medical","g":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms"},{"id":"tzees6","t":"Diagnostic overshadowing","d":"<b>Attributing symptoms of a physical illness to a mental illness.</b>","w":7,"r":"medical","g":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms"},{"id":"1npx6k","t":"Mental disorder due to another medical condition","d":"The diagnosis when a mental disturbance is caused by the <b>physiological effects of another medical condition</b>, such as mood disorder due to hypothyroidism or psychotic disorder due to Cushing's disease.","w":7,"r":"medical","g":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms"},{"id":"6smd87","t":"Substance/medication-induced disorders","d":"The diagnosis when a mental disturbance is caused by the <b>physiological effects of a drug or medication</b>.","w":7,"r":"medical","g":"Medical Illnesses with Psychiatric Symptoms"},{"id":"8qp706","t":"Depersonalization","d":"An <b>extremely uncomfortable feeling of being an observer of one's own body or mental processes</b>, with <b>unreality, detachment or unfamiliarity</b> toward parts of the self or the whole self.","w":5,"r":"trauma","g":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders"},{"id":"123e3pi","t":"Derealization","d":"The <b>recurring feeling that one's surroundings are unreal or distant</b>. <b>The focus is on the outside world</b>, as though walking in a fog or behind an invisible veil, sometimes with <b>visual or auditory distortions</b>.","w":5,"r":"trauma","g":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders"},{"id":"1cx4a8j","t":"Trauma-Informed Care","d":"A framework that involves <b>recognizing and responding to the effects of all types of trauma, recognizing its signs and symptoms</b>, and <b>actively avoiding re-traumatization</b>.","w":5,"r":"trauma","g":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders"},{"id":"1v57aew","t":"Acute stress disorder","d":"A trauma response diagnosed <b>3 days to 1 month</b> after the traumatic event, with an emphasis on <b>dissociative symptoms</b>.","w":5,"r":"trauma","g":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders"},{"id":"1yb9q8h","t":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)","d":"<b>Persistent re-experiencing of a highly traumatic event</b>, with symptoms lasting <b>longer than 1 month</b>.","w":5,"r":"trauma","g":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders"},{"id":"1g0pnag","t":"Anxiety","d":"A feeling of <b>apprehension, uneasiness, uncertainty or dread</b> resulting from a <b>real or perceived threat</b>. A universal human experience and the most basic of emotions.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"197nct0","t":"Anxiety disorder","d":"A condition of <b>persistent anxiety that cannot be dismissed</b>, where coping is ineffective and the anxiety <b>interferes with activities of daily living</b>. Symptoms cluster around <b>excessive, irrational fear and dread</b>.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"y50qqe","t":"Mild anxiety","d":"<b>Heightened perceptual field</b>, sharper observation, and able to <b>work effectively toward a goal</b>. Part of everyday living and adaptive.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"nnf5yd","t":"Moderate anxiety","d":"<b>Narrowed perceptual field</b> with selective inattention. Solves problems <b>but not optimally</b>, and <b>can still follow directions</b>. Sympathetic nervous system symptoms begin.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"11kjzus","t":"Severe anxiety","d":"<b>Greatly reduced and distorted perceptual field</b>, focused on one detail. <b>Problem solving feels impossible</b>. Sense of impending doom and purposeless activity.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"qns3o7","t":"Panic","d":"<b>The most extreme level.</b> Unable to process the environment, completely unable to problem solve, with <b>terror</b>, possible <b>depersonalization or derealization</b>, and hallucinations or delusions.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"1gskqbh","t":"Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD)","d":"<b>Excessive, persistent worry</b> about several events or activities <b>more days than not for at least 6 months</b>, which the person finds difficult to control.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"khfoy8","t":"Separation anxiety disorder","d":"<b>Developmentally inappropriate concern over being away from a significant other</b>, with fear that something horrible will cause permanent separation.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"snnmv4","t":"Specific phobias","d":"A <b>persistent irrational fear of a specific object, activity or situation</b> that is out of proportion to the danger and leads to <b>avoidance</b>.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"12mttpn","t":"Agoraphobia","d":"Intense fear of places or situations where <b>escape might be difficult or embarrassing</b> or <b>help might not be available</b>. The most severe and persistent phobic disorder.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"1x26sul","t":"Social anxiety disorder","d":"Severe anxiety or fear provoked by a <b>social or performance situation that could be evaluated negatively by others</b>. Also called social phobia.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"1ao2d7t","t":"Panic disorder","d":"A <b>chronic condition with exacerbations</b>, characterized by <b>panic attacks</b>: sudden extreme apprehension with impending doom, coming \"out of the blue\" and lasting minutes.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"18y2kn7","t":"Obsession","d":"An <b>unwanted, intrusive, persistent thought, impulse or image</b> that recurs and <b>cannot be dismissed</b> despite trying.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"1fus9v8","t":"Compulsions","d":"<b>Behaviors performed repeatedly, in a ritualistic fashion</b>, to prevent or relieve the anxiety obsessions cause. Relief is <b>only temporary</b>, so the act is repeated.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"1bgjzzq","t":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)","d":"<b>Repetitive unwanted thoughts</b> and <b>repeated rituals</b> that are time-consuming and cause significant distress or impairment.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"w7bahi","t":"Body dysmorphic disorder","d":"<b>Preoccupation with perceived flaws in appearance</b> that others cannot see or find slight, driving checking and camouflaging behaviors.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"thttro","t":"Exposure therapy","d":"Exposing the person to the feared object or obsession. In <b>exposure and response prevention</b>, a component of CBT for OCD, the person is exposed gradually and learns to <b>resist the compulsive ritual</b>.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"9puc4r","t":"Systematic desensitization","d":"<b>Gradual</b> introduction to the feared object in steps <b>from least to most frightening</b>, using a <b>relaxation technique at each step</b>.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"e25sao","t":"Flooding","d":"Exposure to a <b>large amount of the undesirable stimulus</b> to extinguish the anxiety response, so the person learns through <b>prolonged exposure</b> that survival is possible.","w":5,"r":"anxiety","g":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"},{"id":"9hxqrp","t":"Paranoia","d":"<b>Irrational fear</b> ranging from mild to profound, with <b>deep mistrust or suspicion of others</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"1bfh2pg","t":"Delusions","d":"<b>False beliefs held despite a lack of evidence</b>, not corrected by reasoning.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"1gv53f1","t":"Persecutory","d":"Belief that one is being <b>watched, plotted against or ridiculed</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"149n9xt","t":"Referential","d":"Belief that <b>unconnected events are somehow related to you</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"jledh4","t":"Grandiose","d":"Belief that one is a <b>powerful or important person</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"ki62qq","t":"Somatic","d":"Belief that <b>the body is changing in unusual ways</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"jxn1s","t":"Hallucinations","d":"<b>Perceiving a sensory experience for which no external source exists.</b> Can involve all five senses.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"s83fcv","t":"Auditory","d":"<b>Hearing voices or sounds.</b> The most common type.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"a6er5v","t":"Command","d":"Auditory hallucinations that <b>instruct the patient to act in a specific way</b>, from innocuous to dangerous. <b>Suicidal or homicidal commands require immediate safety measures.</b>","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"rewq3u","t":"Visual · olfactory · gustatory · tactile","d":"<b>Seeing</b> people or things · <b>smelling</b> odors · <b>experiencing tastes</b> · <b>feeling body sensations</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"a3i8rv","t":"Circumstantiality","d":"<b>Extremely detailed, lengthy conversation that eventually reaches the point.</b>","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"vlivhx","t":"Tangentiality","d":"<b>Wandering off topic and never reaching the point.</b>","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"e72l6n","t":"Looseness of association","d":"<b>Absence of the normal connectedness</b> of thoughts, ideas and topics.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"19f1zvd","t":"Flight of ideas","d":"Topic changes <b>repeatedly and rapidly with only superficial connections</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"g2zk5v","t":"Pressured speech","d":"<b>Urgent or intense speech</b>, with reluctance to allow comments from others.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"36371u","t":"Thought blocking","d":"<b>Reduction or stoppage of thought</b>, losing the thread mid-sentence.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"tiv2aa","t":"Thought insertion","d":"Belief that <b>someone else has inserted thoughts into the patient's brain</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"19jhxvf","t":"Thought deletion","d":"Belief that <b>thoughts have been taken or are missing</b>.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"4sqczc","t":"Affective blunting","d":"<b>Reduced or constricted affect.</b>","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"10rl4yn","t":"Alogia","d":"<b>Reduction in speech</b>, or poverty of speech.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"1488tkp","t":"Anhedonia","d":"<b>Reduced ability or inability to experience pleasure or joy.</b>","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"y9u5cx","t":"Apathy","d":"<b>Decreased interest in activities or beliefs</b> that would otherwise matter.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"1p8pdpi","t":"Asociality","d":"<b>Decreased desire for social interaction</b>, or discomfort during it.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"5fckzb","t":"Avolition","d":"<b>Reduced or lost motivation or goal-directed behavior.</b>","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"dq9tot","t":"Anosognosia","d":"<b>Inability to realize one is ill</b>, caused by the illness itself. Leads to <b>resistance to or cessation of treatment</b>, often combined with paranoia.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"16xx1a3","t":"Concrete thinking","d":"<b>Impaired ability to think abstractly</b>, interpreting things literally.","w":4,"r":"schizophrenia","g":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"},{"id":"1vhi2l9","t":"Blood alcohol concentration (BAC)","d":"Alcohol intoxication is <b>80 to 100 mg ethanol per deciliter of blood (mg/dL)</b>, also written <b>0.08 to 0.10 g/dL</b>. The Kentucky DUI threshold is <b>0.08</b>.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"sp42of","t":"Countertransference","d":"When a healthcare worker <b>unconsciously displaces feelings related to significant figures in their own past onto the patient</b>. Prevented by self-assessment of one's own attitudes about substance use.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"1fs9ahy","t":"Delirium tremens (DTs)","d":"The <b>most severe form of alcohol withdrawal</b>: a medical emergency that can result in death. Appears any time within the first 72 hours after the last drink. Hepatitis or pancreatitis increases the likelihood.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"1suvgrc","t":"Wernicke encephalopathy","d":"The <b>acute, reversible</b> first stage of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, from thiamine (B1) deficiency. Confusion, ataxia, double vision, droopy eyelid, nystagmus. Treated with large IV thiamine doses 2–3 times daily for 1–2 weeks.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"ji2evs","t":"Korsakoff syndrome","d":"The <b>chronic, debilitating and not typically reversible</b> second stage. Adds severe, irreversible memory impairment, confabulation, hallucinations and repetitious speech. Thiamine replacement for 3–12 months, with a <b>recovery rate of only about 20%</b>.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"1qdbs6m","t":"Dual diagnosis","d":"<b>Co-occurring mental illness and a substance or addictive disorder.</b> Distinct from comorbidity, which is any two or more co-occurring chronic disorders.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"1a3h5x","t":"SBIRT","d":"<b>Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment</b>: early intervention and treatment for people with a substance use disorder <b>in general, not alcohol alone</b>, and those at risk. Usable by any healthcare professional in any setting. Goal: reduce and prevent related health consequences, disease, accidents and injuries, and reduce costs and healthcare utilization.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"ri77sg","t":"AUDIT","d":"<b>Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test</b>, a 10-item WHO tool covering alcohol consumption, drinking behaviors and alcohol-related problems. <b>Used for adults</b>. CRAFFT is the adolescent tool.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"7ju9dh","t":"CRAFFT","d":"Six questions for <b>ages 12–21</b>: <b>C</b>ar, <b>R</b>elax, <b>A</b>lone, <b>F</b>orget, <b>F</b>amily or friends, <b>T</b>rouble. A score of 2 or more needs further evaluation. 4 or more suggests dependence.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"10oh7vp","t":"CAGE","d":"<b>C</b>ut down · <b>A</b>nnoyed by criticism · <b>G</b>uilty about drinking · <b>E</b>ye-opener, a morning drink. A score of <b>2 or more is significant</b>. A score of 1 requires further assessment.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"jygcye","t":"CAGE-AID","d":"The same four questions <b>Adapted to Include Drugs</b>, not alcohol alone.","w":3,"r":"sud","g":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"},{"id":"bqztsm","t":"Mania","d":"At least <b>1 week</b> of elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased activity or energy, causing <b>marked impairment</b> in functioning or requiring hospitalization. <b>Psychotic features may be present.</b>","w":2,"r":"bipolar","g":"Bipolar & Related Disorders"},{"id":"smbqzc","t":"Hypomania","d":"At least <b>4 consecutive days</b> of the same change, uncharacteristic of the person and observable by others, but <b>not</b> severe enough to cause marked impairment or require hospitalization. <b>No psychotic features</b>. Any psychosis makes the episode manic by definition.","w":2,"r":"bipolar","g":"Bipolar & Related Disorders"},{"id":"u6ygdl","t":"Lability","d":"Rapid, exaggerated shifts in emotional expression: the mood switches back and forth, typically every one or two hours.","w":2,"r":"depressive","g":"Depressive Disorders"},{"id":"19l5ee2","t":"Anhedonia","d":"Loss of interest or pleasure in almost all activities. From <i>an</i> (\"without\") + <i>hedone</i> (\"pleasure\"). One of the two symptoms at least one of which must be present for major depressive disorder.","w":2,"r":"depressive","g":"Depressive Disorders"},{"id":"1bu7snl","t":"Psychomotor retardation","d":"Visible slowing of physical activity: movement or speech. Slow talking, long pauses before speaking, taking a long time to cross a room, slow chewing. Associated with <b>severe depression</b>.","w":2,"r":"depressive","g":"Depressive Disorders"},{"id":"xjt1f6","t":"Psychomotor agitation","d":"An increase in activity brought on by mental tension. Restlessness, pacing, tapping fingers or feet, abruptly starting and stopping tasks. Associated with <b>agitated depression</b>.","w":2,"r":"depressive","g":"Depressive Disorders"},{"id":"er5dn","t":"Anger","d":"An emotional response to frustration of desires, a threat to one's needs, or a challenge, from mild irritation to intense fury. A normal emotion, capable of being under personal control. A problem only when expressed aggressively.","w":1,"r":"anger","g":"Anger, Aggression & Violence"},{"id":"9dpcrc","t":"Aggression","d":"Verbal or physical attack intended to threaten or injure the victim's security or self-esteem. Virtually always designed to punish, but <b>not always inappropriate</b>, and sometimes necessary for self-protection.","w":1,"r":"anger","g":"Anger, Aggression & Violence"},{"id":"xy4gct","t":"Violence","d":"Intentional use of force that results in, or could result in, injury to another. <b>Always an objectionable act</b>. That is what separates it from aggression.","w":1,"r":"anger","g":"Anger, Aggression & Violence"},{"id":"otgt58","t":"Restraint","d":"Any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material, or equipment that immobilizes or reduces a patient's ability to move arms, legs, body, or head freely.","w":1,"r":"anger","g":"Anger, Aggression & Violence"},{"id":"921glv","t":"Seclusion","d":"Involuntary confinement of a patient alone in a room they are physically prevented from leaving. Less restrictive than restraints and never punitive. Only for violent or self-destructive behavior that jeopardizes immediate physical safety.","w":1,"r":"anger","g":"Anger, Aggression & Violence"},{"id":"13b3m8c","t":"Critical incident debriefing","d":"Immediate and mandatory after a restraint episode, with patient and staff.","w":1,"r":"anger","g":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","s":1}],"lineups":[{"id":"odd-ied-conduct","row":"childhood","title":"The Three Impulse Control Disorders","shape":"rows","heads":["Disorder","Age of Onset","Gender","Clinical Features","Notes"],"items":[{"name":"Oppositional defiant disorder","points":[{"k":"Age of Onset","h":"Childhood or adolescence. <b>Usually diagnosed around age 8.</b>"},{"k":"Gender","h":"More males"},{"k":"Clinical Features","h":"Angry or irritable mood · argumentative, defiant behavior · vindictiveness · <b>despite the behavior, recognizes that others have rights and that there are rules</b>"},{"k":"Notes","h":"<b>May become conduct disorder</b> in later years."}],"label":"ODD"},{"name":"Intermittent explosive disorder","points":[{"k":"Age of Onset","h":"May be diagnosed at 6. <b>Mean onset 13 to 21 years.</b>"},{"k":"Gender","h":"More males"},{"k":"Clinical Features","h":"Impulsive, unwarranted emotional outbursts · violence · destruction of property"},{"k":"Notes","h":"<b>Early treatment may prevent worsening pathology.</b>"}],"label":"Intermittent explosive"},{"name":"Conduct disorder","points":[{"k":"Age of Onset","h":"<b>Childhood onset (before 10): worse prognosis.</b> Adolescent onset: no symptoms before 10."},{"k":"Gender","h":"Childhood onset: more males. Adolescent onset: equal."},{"k":"Clinical Features","h":"Unimpulsive violation of the rights of others · aggression to people and animals · destruction of property · deceitfulness · rules violation"},{"k":"Notes","h":"More criminal involvement. <b>May be a precursor to antisocial personality disorder.</b>"}],"label":"Conduct disorder"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"k":"Clinical Features","h":"Angry or irritable mood · argumentative, defiant behavior · vindictiveness · <b>despite the behavior, recognizes that others have rights and that there are rules</b>"},{"i":0,"k":"Notes","h":"<b>May become conduct disorder</b> in later years."},{"i":1,"k":"Clinical Features","h":"Impulsive, unwarranted emotional outbursts · violence · destruction of property"},{"i":2,"k":"Clinical Features","h":"Unimpulsive violation of the rights of others · aggression to people and animals · destruction of property · deceitfulness · rules violation"},{"i":2,"k":"Notes","h":"More criminal involvement. <b>May be a precursor to antisocial personality disorder.</b>"}],"src":{"href":"week6-impulse-control-disorders.html#overview","title":"Impulse Control Disorders"}},{"id":"adhd-types","row":"childhood","title":"ADHD: Hyperactive-Impulsive or Inattentive","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Hyperactivity-Impulsivity Type","points":[{"k":"","h":"Fidgets, squirms, <b>can't sit still</b>. Leaves seat before excused."},{"k":"","h":"Runs about or climbs excessively or at inappropriate times."},{"k":"","h":"Difficulty playing quietly, such as board games."},{"k":"","h":"Always <b>\"on the go,\" \"driven by a motor\"</b>. Talks excessively."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Blurts answers</b> before questions are finished. Speaks before thinking."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Interrupts or intrudes</b> on conversations or games."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Problem waiting for their turn.</b>"}],"label":"Hyperactivity-Impulsivity Type"},{"name":"Inattentive Type","points":[{"k":"","h":"Misses details or makes <b>repeated careless mistakes</b>."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Trouble keeping attention</b> on tasks or play. Easily distracted."},{"k":"","h":"Often <b>does not seem to listen</b> when spoken to directly."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Does not follow through</b> to complete tasks."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Trouble organizing</b> activities. Disorganized."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Avoids tasks requiring mental effort.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Loses things</b>, forgetful, easily bored."}],"label":"Inattentive Type"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"Fidgets, squirms, <b>can't sit still</b>. Leaves seat before excused."},{"i":0,"h":"Runs about or climbs excessively or at inappropriate times."},{"i":0,"h":"Difficulty playing quietly, such as board games."},{"i":0,"h":"Always <b>\"on the go,\" \"driven by a motor\"</b>. Talks excessively."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Blurts answers</b> before questions are finished. Speaks before thinking."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Interrupts or intrudes</b> on conversations or games."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Problem waiting for their turn.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"Misses details or makes <b>repeated careless mistakes</b>."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Trouble keeping attention</b> on tasks or play. Easily distracted."},{"i":1,"h":"Often <b>does not seem to listen</b> when spoken to directly."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Does not follow through</b> to complete tasks."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Trouble organizing</b> activities. Disorganized."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Avoids tasks requiring mental effort.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Loses things</b>, forgetful, easily bored."}],"src":{"href":"week6-neurodevelopmental-disorders.html#adhd","title":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders"}},{"id":"delirium-dementia","row":"neurocognitive","title":"Delirium or Dementia","shape":"cols","heads":["Aspect","Delirium","Dementia"],"items":[{"name":"Delirium","points":[{"k":"Definition","h":"<b>Acute confusion</b> with fluctuating consciousness."},{"k":"Onset","h":"Acute and rapid, over <b>hours to days</b>."},{"k":"Duration","h":"Short-term, reversible with treatment."},{"k":"Cause","h":"Typically triggered by an <b>underlying issue</b> such as infection, medications or dehydration."},{"k":"Symptoms","h":"Confusion, altered attention, disorientation, hallucinations, disrupted sleep."},{"k":"Level of consciousness","h":"<b>Fluctuates</b>. May be drowsy or hyperactive."},{"k":"Memory","h":"Short-term memory affected. May not remember events."},{"k":"Behavioral changes","h":"Agitation, restlessness, paranoia, mood swings."},{"k":"Reversibility","h":"<b>Often reversible</b> with treatment of the underlying cause."}],"label":"Delirium"},{"name":"Dementia","points":[{"k":"Definition","h":"<b>Chronic cognitive decline</b> affecting memory, thinking and behavior."},{"k":"Onset","h":"Gradual, over <b>months to years</b>."},{"k":"Duration","h":"Long-term, progressive and irreversible."},{"k":"Cause","h":"<b>Degenerative diseases</b> such as Alzheimer's disease and vascular dementia."},{"k":"Symptoms","h":"Memory loss, poor judgment, language difficulties, difficulty with daily tasks."},{"k":"Level of consciousness","h":"<b>Generally clear</b>, but can become impaired in later stages."},{"k":"Memory","h":"Short-term memory affected early on. Long-term memory may be impaired in later stages."},{"k":"Behavioral changes","h":"Withdrawn, apathetic, repetitive behaviors, poor decision-making."},{"k":"Reversibility","h":"<b>Progressive and irreversible.</b>"}],"label":"Dementia"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"k":"Definition","h":"<b>Acute confusion</b> with fluctuating consciousness."},{"i":0,"k":"Onset","h":"Acute and rapid, over <b>hours to days</b>."},{"i":0,"k":"Duration","h":"Short-term, reversible with treatment."},{"i":0,"k":"Cause","h":"Typically triggered by an <b>underlying issue</b> such as infection, medications or dehydration."},{"i":0,"k":"Symptoms","h":"Confusion, altered attention, disorientation, hallucinations, disrupted sleep."},{"i":0,"k":"Level of consciousness","h":"<b>Fluctuates</b>. May be drowsy or hyperactive."},{"i":0,"k":"Behavioral changes","h":"Agitation, restlessness, paranoia, mood swings."},{"i":0,"k":"Reversibility","h":"<b>Often reversible</b> with treatment of the underlying cause."},{"i":1,"k":"Definition","h":"<b>Chronic cognitive decline</b> affecting memory, thinking and behavior."},{"i":1,"k":"Onset","h":"Gradual, over <b>months to years</b>."},{"i":1,"k":"Duration","h":"Long-term, progressive and irreversible."},{"i":1,"k":"Symptoms","h":"Memory loss, poor judgment, language difficulties, difficulty with daily tasks."},{"i":1,"k":"Level of consciousness","h":"<b>Generally clear</b>, but can become impaired in later stages."},{"i":1,"k":"Behavioral changes","h":"Withdrawn, apathetic, repetitive behaviors, poor decision-making."},{"i":1,"k":"Reversibility","h":"<b>Progressive and irreversible.</b>"}],"src":{"href":"week7-neurocognitive-disorders.html#delirium-vs-dementia","title":"Neurocognitive Disorders"}},{"id":"aging-or-alzheimers","row":"neurocognitive","title":"Normal Aging or Alzheimer's Disease","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Typical Age-Related Changes","points":[{"k":"","h":"Sometimes forgetting names or appointments, but remembering them later."},{"k":"","h":"Making occasional errors when balancing a checkbook."},{"k":"","h":"Occasionally needing help to use the settings on a microwave or to record a television show."},{"k":"","h":"Forgetting the day of the week but figuring it out later."},{"k":"","h":"Vision changes related to cataracts."},{"k":"","h":"Sometimes having difficulty finding the correct word."},{"k":"","h":"Misplacing things from time to time and retracing steps to find them."},{"k":"","h":"Making a bad decision once in a while."},{"k":"","h":"Sometimes feeling weary of work, family and social obligations."},{"k":"","h":"Developing specific ways of doing things and becoming irritable when routine is disrupted."}],"label":"Normal aging"},{"name":"Signs of Alzheimer's Disease","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Memory loss that disrupts daily life.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Challenges in planning or solving problems.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Difficulty completing familiar tasks.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Confusion with time or place.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Trouble understanding visual images or spatial relationships.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>New problems with words in speaking or writing.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Misplacing things and losing the ability to retrace steps.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Decreased or poor judgment.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Withdrawal from social and work activities.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Changes in mood and personality.</b>"}],"label":"Alzheimer's disease"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"Sometimes forgetting names or appointments, but remembering them later."},{"i":0,"h":"Making occasional errors when balancing a checkbook."},{"i":0,"h":"Occasionally needing help to use the settings on a microwave or to record a television show."},{"i":0,"h":"Forgetting the day of the week but figuring it out later."},{"i":0,"h":"Vision changes related to cataracts."},{"i":0,"h":"Sometimes having difficulty finding the correct word."},{"i":0,"h":"Misplacing things from time to time and retracing steps to find them."},{"i":0,"h":"Making a bad decision once in a while."},{"i":0,"h":"Sometimes feeling weary of work, family and social obligations."},{"i":0,"h":"Developing specific ways of doing things and becoming irritable when routine is disrupted."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Memory loss that disrupts daily life.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Challenges in planning or solving problems.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Difficulty completing familiar tasks.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Confusion with time or place.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Trouble understanding visual images or spatial relationships.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>New problems with words in speaking or writing.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Misplacing things and losing the ability to retrace steps.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Decreased or poor judgment.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Withdrawal from social and work activities.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Changes in mood and personality.</b>"}],"src":{"href":"week7-neurocognitive-disorders.html#alzheimers","title":"Neurocognitive Disorders"}},{"id":"somatic-six","row":"somatic","title":"The Six Somatic Presentations","shape":"rows","heads":["Disorder","Clinical Manifestations","Impact on the Individual","Treatments"],"items":[{"name":"Somatic symptom disorder","points":[{"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Actual, persistent and distressing physical symptoms</b> (pain, fatigue, digestive issues) <b>that cause significant anxiety</b>.</li>\n        <li><b>Often without a clear medical cause.</b></li>\n        <li>Preoccupation with health, with excessive time and energy devoted to symptoms.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Chronic anxiety and worry about health, interfering with daily life.</li>\n        <li><b>Frequent doctor visits and unnecessary medical tests</b>, with a high emotional and financial burden.</li>\n        <li>Impaired social and occupational functioning.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>CBT</b> for health anxiety and dysfunctional thoughts · <b>mindfulness</b> and relaxation techniques to reduce stress · <b>antidepressants</b>, such as SSRIs, for underlying anxiety or depression."}],"label":"Somatic symptom"},{"name":"Illness anxiety disorder","points":[{"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Intense <b>fear or worry about having or acquiring a serious illness, despite few or no actual physical symptoms</b>.</li>\n        <li>Constantly checks the body for signs of illness and <b>seeks reassurance from doctors despite normal results</b>, or avoids medical situations.</li>\n        <li>Hyperfocus on health-related information, such as medical websites.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Persistent fear or worry causing significant distress and disruption of daily activities.</li>\n        <li><b>Avoids certain activities or places for fear of illness.</b></li>\n        <li>Can lead to <b>doctor shopping</b> and high health-related costs, or to <b>avoiding healthcare altogether</b>.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>CBT</b> to reduce health anxiety and <b>correct misinterpretations of bodily sensations</b> · <b>psychoeducation</b> about health anxiety · <b>antidepressants or anxiolytics</b> to manage anxiety."}],"label":"Illness anxiety"},{"name":"Conversion disorder","points":[{"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Neurological symptoms</b> (paralysis, tremors, non-epileptic seizures, vision problems) <b>that are inconsistent with or cannot be explained by medical tests</b>.</li>\n        <li>Often <b>triggered by stress or trauma</b>.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Sudden disability</b>, with impaired mobility and independence.</li>\n        <li>Emotional distress from the sudden onset and the lack of a clear medical explanation.</li>\n        <li>Social and occupational disruption.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Physical therapy and rehabilitation</b> to help restore function · <b>CBT</b> to address stress or trauma · <b>psychodynamic therapy</b> to explore unresolved psychological conflict · <b>stress management techniques</b> to prevent symptom exacerbation."}],"label":"Conversion"},{"name":"Psychological factors affecting medical condition","points":[{"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Psychological factors</b>, such as stress, anxiety or depression, <b>that worsen a physical medical condition</b> such as chronic pain, asthma or heart disease.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Poor health outcomes</b> from the interaction between psychological and physical symptoms.</li>\n        <li>Difficulty managing multiple health concerns at once.</li>\n        <li>Impaired quality of life.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Integrated care approach</b> combining medical and psychological treatment · <b>CBT</b> for stress and coping strategies · <b>relaxation techniques and mindfulness</b> to reduce the impact of psychological stress on physical health."}],"label":"Psychological factors"},{"name":"Factitious disorder","points":[{"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Intentionally pretending, lying about, faking or producing</b> physical or psychological symptoms <b>to assume the sick role</b> and gain things such as attention or sympathy.</li>\n        <li>May involve falsifying medical histories, manipulating test results or intentionally harming oneself.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Emotional distress stemming from the need for attention or care.</li>\n        <li><b>Trust issues</b> in relationships and among healthcare providers because of the deception.</li>\n        <li><b>Risk of unnecessary medical treatments and complications.</b></li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Psychotherapy</b>, particularly <b>CBT or psychodynamic therapy</b>, to uncover underlying emotional distress · <b>long-term therapy</b> for the psychological need to gain attention through illness · <b>family therapy</b> if symptoms are imposed on others."}],"label":"Factitious"},{"name":"Malingering","points":[{"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Intentionally exaggerating or fabricating</b> physical or psychological symptoms <b>for external gain</b>: avoiding work, admission to a hospital or psychiatric facility, financial compensation, or prescription medications.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Distrust and breakdown in relationships, especially with healthcare providers.</li>\n        <li><b>Legal consequences</b> if fraudulent behavior is detected.</li>\n        <li>Potential for unnecessary treatments or accommodations based on false information.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Psychiatric evaluation</b> to confirm the diagnosis and underlying motivations · <b>legal or social intervention</b> if external gains are discovered · in some cases, <b>behavioral therapy</b> for the underlying motives."}],"label":"Malingering"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Actual, persistent and distressing physical symptoms</b> (pain, fatigue, digestive issues) <b>that cause significant anxiety</b>.</li>\n        <li><b>Often without a clear medical cause.</b></li>\n        <li>Preoccupation with health, with excessive time and energy devoted to symptoms.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":0,"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Chronic anxiety and worry about health, interfering with daily life.</li>\n        <li><b>Frequent doctor visits and unnecessary medical tests</b>, with a high emotional and financial burden.</li>\n        <li>Impaired social and occupational functioning.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":1,"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Intense <b>fear or worry about having or acquiring a serious illness, despite few or no actual physical symptoms</b>.</li>\n        <li>Constantly checks the body for signs of illness and <b>seeks reassurance from doctors despite normal results</b>, or avoids medical situations.</li>\n        <li>Hyperfocus on health-related information, such as medical websites.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":1,"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Persistent fear or worry causing significant distress and disruption of daily activities.</li>\n        <li><b>Avoids certain activities or places for fear of illness.</b></li>\n        <li>Can lead to <b>doctor shopping</b> and high health-related costs, or to <b>avoiding healthcare altogether</b>.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":2,"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Neurological symptoms</b> (paralysis, tremors, non-epileptic seizures, vision problems) <b>that are inconsistent with or cannot be explained by medical tests</b>.</li>\n        <li>Often <b>triggered by stress or trauma</b>.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":2,"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Sudden disability</b>, with impaired mobility and independence.</li>\n        <li>Emotional distress from the sudden onset and the lack of a clear medical explanation.</li>\n        <li>Social and occupational disruption.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":2,"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Physical therapy and rehabilitation</b> to help restore function · <b>CBT</b> to address stress or trauma · <b>psychodynamic therapy</b> to explore unresolved psychological conflict · <b>stress management techniques</b> to prevent symptom exacerbation."},{"i":3,"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Poor health outcomes</b> from the interaction between psychological and physical symptoms.</li>\n        <li>Difficulty managing multiple health concerns at once.</li>\n        <li>Impaired quality of life.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":3,"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Integrated care approach</b> combining medical and psychological treatment · <b>CBT</b> for stress and coping strategies · <b>relaxation techniques and mindfulness</b> to reduce the impact of psychological stress on physical health."},{"i":4,"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Intentionally pretending, lying about, faking or producing</b> physical or psychological symptoms <b>to assume the sick role</b> and gain things such as attention or sympathy.</li>\n        <li>May involve falsifying medical histories, manipulating test results or intentionally harming oneself.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":4,"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Emotional distress stemming from the need for attention or care.</li>\n        <li><b>Trust issues</b> in relationships and among healthcare providers because of the deception.</li>\n        <li><b>Risk of unnecessary medical treatments and complications.</b></li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":4,"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Psychotherapy</b>, particularly <b>CBT or psychodynamic therapy</b>, to uncover underlying emotional distress · <b>long-term therapy</b> for the psychological need to gain attention through illness · <b>family therapy</b> if symptoms are imposed on others."},{"i":5,"k":"Clinical Manifestations","h":"<ul>\n        <li><b>Intentionally exaggerating or fabricating</b> physical or psychological symptoms <b>for external gain</b>: avoiding work, admission to a hospital or psychiatric facility, financial compensation, or prescription medications.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":5,"k":"Impact on the Individual","h":"<ul>\n        <li>Distrust and breakdown in relationships, especially with healthcare providers.</li>\n        <li><b>Legal consequences</b> if fraudulent behavior is detected.</li>\n        <li>Potential for unnecessary treatments or accommodations based on false information.</li>\n      </ul>"},{"i":5,"k":"Treatments","h":"<b>Psychiatric evaluation</b> to confirm the diagnosis and underlying motivations · <b>legal or social intervention</b> if external gains are discovered · in some cases, <b>behavioral therapy</b> for the underlying motives."}],"src":{"href":"week6-somatic-symptom-disorders.html#manifestations","title":"Somatic Symptom Disorders"}},{"id":"anxiety-levels","row":"anxiety","title":"The Four Levels of Anxiety","shape":"rows","heads":["Level","Perceptual Field","Ability to Problem Solve","Physical and Other Characteristics"],"items":[{"name":"Mild","points":[{"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Heightened.</b> Sees, hears and grasps more. Observations sharper, focus flexible, aware of the anxiety."},{"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Works effectively toward a goal</b> and examines alternatives."},{"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"Slight discomfort, attention-seeking, restlessness, easily startled, irritability. Mild tension-relieving behavior: <b>foot or finger tapping, lip chewing, fidgeting</b>. Part of <b>everyday living</b>, adaptive."}],"label":"Mild"},{"name":"Moderate","points":[{"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Narrowed.</b> Some details excluded. <b>Selective inattention</b>: only certain things are seen or heard unless pointed out. Focuses on the source of the anxiety."},{"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Solves problems, but not at the optimal level.</b> <b>Still able to follow directions.</b>"},{"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"<b>Sympathetic nervous system symptoms begin</b>: tension, pounding heart, increased pulse and respirations, perspiration. Mild somatic symptoms: headache, urinary frequency, backache, insomnia. <b>Voice tremors, poor concentration, shaking.</b>"}],"label":"Moderate"},{"name":"Severe","points":[{"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Greatly reduced and distorted.</b> Focuses on one detail or scattered details, and misses events even when they are pointed out."},{"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Feels impossible.</b> Cannot see connections between events. May be <b>dazed and confused</b>."},{"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"Behavior <b>automatic, aimed at relieving anxiety</b>. Dread, purposeless activity, <b>sense of impending doom</b>. Chest discomfort, dizziness, nausea, sleeplessness, <b>diaphoresis</b>. Withdrawal, <b>loud and rapid speech, threats and demands</b>."}],"label":"Severe"},{"name":"Panic","points":[{"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Cannot process the environment.</b> Focus lost. May feel unreal (<b>depersonalization</b>) or that the world is unreal (<b>derealization</b>)."},{"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Completely unable.</b> Disorganized or irrational reasoning."},{"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"<b>Terror.</b> Immobility, severe hyperactivity or flight. Unintelligible speech or inability to speak. Numbness, shortness of breath, chest pain, palpitations. <b>Severe withdrawal, hallucinations or delusions</b>, likely out of touch with reality. <b>The most extreme level.</b>"}],"label":"Panic"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Heightened.</b> Sees, hears and grasps more. Observations sharper, focus flexible, aware of the anxiety."},{"i":0,"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Works effectively toward a goal</b> and examines alternatives."},{"i":1,"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Narrowed.</b> Some details excluded. <b>Selective inattention</b>: only certain things are seen or heard unless pointed out. Focuses on the source of the anxiety."},{"i":1,"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Solves problems, but not at the optimal level.</b> <b>Still able to follow directions.</b>"},{"i":1,"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"<b>Sympathetic nervous system symptoms begin</b>: tension, pounding heart, increased pulse and respirations, perspiration. Mild somatic symptoms: headache, urinary frequency, backache, insomnia. <b>Voice tremors, poor concentration, shaking.</b>"},{"i":2,"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Greatly reduced and distorted.</b> Focuses on one detail or scattered details, and misses events even when they are pointed out."},{"i":2,"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Feels impossible.</b> Cannot see connections between events. May be <b>dazed and confused</b>."},{"i":2,"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"Behavior <b>automatic, aimed at relieving anxiety</b>. Dread, purposeless activity, <b>sense of impending doom</b>. Chest discomfort, dizziness, nausea, sleeplessness, <b>diaphoresis</b>. Withdrawal, <b>loud and rapid speech, threats and demands</b>."},{"i":3,"k":"Perceptual Field","h":"<b>Cannot process the environment.</b> Focus lost. May feel unreal (<b>depersonalization</b>) or that the world is unreal (<b>derealization</b>)."},{"i":3,"k":"Ability to Problem Solve","h":"<b>Completely unable.</b> Disorganized or irrational reasoning."},{"i":3,"k":"Physical and Other Characteristics","h":"<b>Terror.</b> Immobility, severe hyperactivity or flight. Unintelligible speech or inability to speak. Numbness, shortness of breath, chest pain, palpitations. <b>Severe withdrawal, hallucinations or delusions</b>, likely out of touch with reality. <b>The most extreme level.</b>"}],"src":{"href":"week5-anxiety-ocd-disorders.html#levels","title":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"}},{"id":"interventions-by-level","row":"anxiety","title":"Interventions: Mild to Moderate or Severe to Panic","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Mild to Moderate","points":[{"k":"","h":"Help the person <b>identify the anxiety</b> and <b>anticipate</b> what provokes it."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Nonverbal interest</b>: eye contact, how you sit."},{"k":"","h":"Encourage talking about and <b>focus on their feelings and concerns</b>. <b>Clarify</b> what is said."},{"k":"","h":"Identify <b>thoughts and feelings before the onset</b> of anxiety."},{"k":"","h":"Encourage <b>problem-solving</b> and <b>alternative solutions</b>."},{"k":"","h":"Provide <b>outlets for working off excess energy</b>."},{"k":"","h":"Prevent escalation: a <b>calm presence</b>, recognizing distress, <b>willingness to listen</b>, exploring <b>what relieved anxiety in the past</b>, and offering alternative coping strategies."}],"label":"Mild to Moderate"},{"name":"Severe to Panic","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Provide safety in all areas</b>, personal and environmental: warmth, fluids, elimination, pain relief, family."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Calm manner. Clear, simple statements</b>, repeated if necessary. <b>Low-pitched voice</b>, speaking slowly."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Move to a quiet environment</b> with minimal stimulation."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Reinforce reality</b> if distortions such as delusions or hallucinations are present."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Assess the need for medication.</b>"}],"label":"Severe to Panic"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"Help the person <b>identify the anxiety</b> and <b>anticipate</b> what provokes it."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Nonverbal interest</b>: eye contact, how you sit."},{"i":0,"h":"Encourage talking about and <b>focus on their feelings and concerns</b>. <b>Clarify</b> what is said."},{"i":0,"h":"Identify <b>thoughts and feelings before the onset</b> of anxiety."},{"i":0,"h":"Encourage <b>problem-solving</b> and <b>alternative solutions</b>."},{"i":0,"h":"Provide <b>outlets for working off excess energy</b>."},{"i":0,"h":"Prevent escalation: a <b>calm presence</b>, recognizing distress, <b>willingness to listen</b>, exploring <b>what relieved anxiety in the past</b>, and offering alternative coping strategies."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Provide safety in all areas</b>, personal and environmental: warmth, fluids, elimination, pain relief, family."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Calm manner. Clear, simple statements</b>, repeated if necessary. <b>Low-pitched voice</b>, speaking slowly."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Move to a quiet environment</b> with minimal stimulation."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Reinforce reality</b> if distortions such as delusions or hallucinations are present."}],"src":{"href":"week5-anxiety-ocd-disorders.html#interventions","title":"Anxiety & OCD Disorders"}},{"id":"delusions-do-do-not","row":"schizophrenia","title":"Delusions and Hallucinations: Do or Do Not","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Do","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Remain calm, acknowledge and accept</b> the patient's experience and feelings."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Focus on helping the patient feel safe</b>, addressing the underlying theme of fear."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Ask the patient to describe the delusion</b>, and assess its <b>intensity, frequency and duration</b>."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Validate the part that is real and present reality.</b> Identify <b>what triggered it</b>."},{"k":"","h":"As reality testing improves, <b>supportively convey doubt</b> and <b>clarify misinterpretations of the environment</b>."}],"label":"Do"},{"name":"Do Not","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Never debate or argue the delusional content.</b> It interferes with the development of trust."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Do not focus on the delusion itself.</b> Focusing on the fear and what helps the patient feel secure is what is therapeutic."},{"k":"","h":"Until reality testing improves, <b>never try to prove the delusion is incorrect</b>. This <b>intensifies the delusion</b> and makes staff appear untrustworthy."}],"label":"Do Not"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"<b>Remain calm, acknowledge and accept</b> the patient's experience and feelings."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Focus on helping the patient feel safe</b>, addressing the underlying theme of fear."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Ask the patient to describe the delusion</b>, and assess its <b>intensity, frequency and duration</b>."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Validate the part that is real and present reality.</b> Identify <b>what triggered it</b>."},{"i":0,"h":"As reality testing improves, <b>supportively convey doubt</b> and <b>clarify misinterpretations of the environment</b>."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Never debate or argue the delusional content.</b> It interferes with the development of trust."},{"i":1,"h":"Until reality testing improves, <b>never try to prove the delusion is incorrect</b>. This <b>intensifies the delusion</b> and makes staff appear untrustworthy."}],"src":{"href":"week4-schizophrenia.html#nursing-perception","title":"Schizophrenia & Schizoaffective Disorder"}},{"id":"wernicke-korsakoff","row":"sud","title":"Wernicke Encephalopathy or Korsakoff Syndrome","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Wernicke's Encephalopathy","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Acute and reversible.</b> May clear within a few weeks, or progress to Korsakoff syndrome."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Treatment: thiamine replacement in large doses, IV, 2–3 times daily for 1–2 weeks</b>, typically about 10 days."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Symptoms:</b> confusion · <b>ataxia</b>: loss of muscle coordination, posture and balance problems, tremors · <b>double vision, droopy eyelid, nystagmus</b> (abnormal eye movements)."}],"label":"Wernicke"},{"name":"Korsakoff Syndrome","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Chronic and debilitating</b>, and <b>not typically reversible</b>."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Treatment: thiamine replacement for 3–12 months</b>, varying with symptoms, severity and whether psychosis is present."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Outcome: most never fully recover, with a recovery rate of only about 20%</b>, though cognition may improve with thiamine and nutritional support. Many require residential care."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Symptoms:</b> everything seen in Wernicke's, plus <b>severe, irreversible, persistent memory impairment</b>, including <b>anterograde amnesia</b>, difficulty forming new memories · <b>confabulation</b>, filling memory gaps with invented detail rather than lying · hallucinations, auditory and visual · repetitious speech and actions · problems with decision-making, planning, organizing and completing tasks."}],"label":"Korsakoff"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"<b>Treatment: thiamine replacement in large doses, IV, 2–3 times daily for 1–2 weeks</b>, typically about 10 days."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Symptoms:</b> confusion · <b>ataxia</b>: loss of muscle coordination, posture and balance problems, tremors · <b>double vision, droopy eyelid, nystagmus</b> (abnormal eye movements)."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Chronic and debilitating</b>, and <b>not typically reversible</b>."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Treatment: thiamine replacement for 3–12 months</b>, varying with symptoms, severity and whether psychosis is present."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Outcome: most never fully recover, with a recovery rate of only about 20%</b>, though cognition may improve with thiamine and nutritional support. Many require residential care."}],"src":{"href":"week3-substance-use-disorders.html#wernicke-korsakoff","title":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"}},{"id":"opioid-intoxication-overdose","row":"sud","title":"Opioid Intoxication or Overdose","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Opioid Intoxication","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Pinpoint pupils (miosis)</b>, a major sign."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Bradycardia · hypotension · hypothermia.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Sedation</b> and <b>calmness</b>. <b>Head nodding</b>."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Hypokinesis</b>: slowed movement. <b>Slurred speech</b>."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Altered mood</b>: euphoria."}],"label":"Intoxication"},{"name":"Recognizing an Overdose","points":[{"k":"","h":"Drowsy, difficult to wake, or unconscious."},{"k":"","h":"Respirations <b>slow, shallow or absent</b>."},{"k":"","h":"Nails and/or lips <b>blue or purple</b>. Skin pale, cool and clammy."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Choking, snoring or gurgling sounds.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"<b>Pinpoint pupils.</b>"},{"k":"","h":"The three cardinal signs: <b>coma, pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression.</b>"}],"label":"Overdose"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"<b>Pinpoint pupils (miosis)</b>, a major sign."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Bradycardia · hypotension · hypothermia.</b>"},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Sedation</b> and <b>calmness</b>. <b>Head nodding</b>."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Hypokinesis</b>: slowed movement. <b>Slurred speech</b>."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Altered mood</b>: euphoria."},{"i":1,"h":"Drowsy, difficult to wake, or unconscious."},{"i":1,"h":"Respirations <b>slow, shallow or absent</b>."},{"i":1,"h":"Nails and/or lips <b>blue or purple</b>. Skin pale, cool and clammy."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Choking, snoring or gurgling sounds.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"The three cardinal signs: <b>coma, pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression.</b>"}],"src":{"href":"week3-substance-use-disorders.html#oud","title":"Substance Use & Addictive Disorders"}},{"id":"manic-hypomanic","row":"bipolar","title":"Manic Episode or Hypomanic Episode","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Manic Episode","points":[{"k":"","h":"At least <b>1 week</b> of elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased activity or energy."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Marked impairment</b> in social or occupational functioning, <i>or</i> hospitalization required."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Psychotic features may be present.</b>"}],"label":"Manic"},{"name":"Hypomanic Episode","points":[{"k":"","h":"At least <b>4 consecutive days</b>, present most of the day, nearly every day."},{"k":"","h":"An unequivocal change in functioning that is <b>uncharacteristic of the person and observable by others</b>."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Not</b> severe enough to cause marked impairment or to require hospitalization."},{"k":"","h":"<b>No psychotic features</b>. If there are any, the episode is by definition manic."}],"label":"Hypomanic"}],"drill":[{"i":0,"h":"At least <b>1 week</b> of elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased activity or energy."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Marked impairment</b> in social or occupational functioning, <i>or</i> hospitalization required."},{"i":0,"h":"<b>Psychotic features may be present.</b>"},{"i":1,"h":"At least <b>4 consecutive days</b>, present most of the day, nearly every day."},{"i":1,"h":"An unequivocal change in functioning that is <b>uncharacteristic of the person and observable by others</b>."},{"i":1,"h":"<b>Not</b> severe enough to cause marked impairment or to require hospitalization."}],"src":{"href":"week2-bipolar-disorders.html#hypomania","title":"Bipolar & Related Disorders"}}],"refs":[{"id":"speech-language","row":"childhood","title":"Speech Disorders or Language Disorders","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Speech Disorders","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Problems making sounds</b> and in vocalization."}],"label":"Speech Disorders"},{"name":"Language Disorders","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Expressive language disorder</b>: inability to develop the skills to communicate verbally or through sign language."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Social communication disorder</b>: problems communicating socially, such as frequently interrupting or being minimally verbal."}],"label":"Language Disorders"}],"src":{"href":"week6-neurodevelopmental-disorders.html#overview","title":"Neurodevelopmental Disorders"}},{"id":"alzheimer-stages","row":"neurocognitive","title":"The Stages of Alzheimer's Disease","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Mild","points":[{"k":"","h":"Forgetfulness, misplaced articles, decreased recall, social withdrawal, frustration with self. Changes may not be apparent to others, and memory tests are still done fairly well."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Priority: safety, and delay cognitive decline.</b>"}],"label":"Mild"},{"name":"Moderate","points":[{"k":"","h":"Decreased self-care and way-finding, disoriented to time and place, wandering, pacing, possible hallucinations or delusions, decreased visual perception leading to accidents, emotional lability. Needs supervision, and symptoms are noticeable."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Priority: protect the patient from hurting self</b>, such as by wandering."}],"label":"Moderate"},{"name":"Severe","points":[{"k":"","h":"Cannot care for self, loses the use of language, minimal long-term memory. Incontinence, gait disturbance, sometimes seizures. Constant, complete care."},{"k":"","h":"<b>Priority: physical needs become the focus of care</b>: nutrition, hygiene, self-care."}],"label":"Severe"}],"src":{"href":"week7-neurocognitive-disorders.html#alzheimers","title":"Neurocognitive Disorders"}},{"id":"somatic-primary-feature","row":"somatic","title":"What Sets Each Disorder Apart","shape":"pairs","heads":["Disorder","What Sets It Apart"],"items":[{"name":"Conversion disorder","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Loss of voluntary motor or sensory functioning</b> is the primary feature."}],"label":"Conversion disorder"},{"name":"Somatic symptom disorder","points":[{"k":"","h":"Presents with <b>exaggerated, inconsistent, yet complicated medical histories</b>."}],"label":"Somatic symptom disorder"},{"name":"Body dysmorphic disorder","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Preoccupation with an imagined defect in appearance.</b>"}],"label":"Body dysmorphic disorder"},{"name":"Illness anxiety disorder (formerly hypochondriasis)","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Fears having a serious disease despite no significant pathology</b> or other reason for thinking so."}],"label":"Illness anxiety disorder (formerly hypochondriasis)"}],"src":{"href":"week6-somatic-symptom-disorders.html#telling-apart","title":"Somatic Symptom Disorders"}},{"id":"hyper-hypoarousal","row":"trauma","title":"Hyperarousal or Hypoarousal","shape":"cards","heads":[],"items":[{"name":"Hyperarousal","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Intense fear, aggression, anxiety.</b>"}],"label":"Hyperarousal"},{"name":"Hypoarousal","points":[{"k":"","h":"<b>Withdrawal, numbness, isolation.</b>"}],"label":"Hypoarousal"}],"src":{"href":"week5-trauma-stressor-disorders.html#children","title":"Trauma & Stressor Disorders"}}]};
